OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026", "title": "A Direct Proof of Optimal Max-Cut Hardness", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "102", "family_title": "The Unique Games Conjecture and optimal approximation thresholds", "abstract_html": "We prove that approximating Max-Cut on simple unweighted graphs within any fixed factor greater than the Goemans–Williamson constant is NP-hard.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}<\\alpha\\le 1\\), it is NP-hard to approximate Max-Cut within a factor of \\(\\alpha\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a graph \\(G\\) with nonnegative edge weights, \\(\\operatorname{MaxCut}(G)\\) is the maximum weight of edges crossing a partition of its vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">An \\(\\alpha\\)-approximation for this maximization problem returns a feasible cut of weight at least \\(\\alpha\\operatorname{MaxCut}(G)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the Goemans–Williamson constant by\n  \\[\n  \\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}\n  =\n  \\min_{-1\\le \\rho&lt;1}\n  \\frac{2\\arccos\\rho}{\\pi(1-\\rho)}\n  =\n  0.878567\\ldots .\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}&lt;\\alpha\\le 1\\), it is NP-hard to approximate Max-Cut within a factor of \\(\\alpha\\). This holds for simple unweighted graphs. Thus a polynomial-time algorithm always returning such a cut would imply \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Substituting \\(\\rho=-t\\) in the definition of \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}\n=\n\\min_{-1&lt;t\\le1}\n\\frac{1+B(t)}{1+t},\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(B(t)=2\\arcsin(t)/\\pi\\) also defines the expression for negative \\(t\\). For \\(-1&lt;t\\le0\\), the ratio is at least one; for \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\), it is less than one. At \\(t=1\\) it equals one, and as \\(t\\downarrow-1\\) it diverges. Its minimum therefore occurs in \\((0,1)\\).</p><p>Fix \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}&lt;\\alpha\\le1\\). By continuity and density of rationals, choose rational \\(t\\in(0,1)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\alpha\\frac{1+t}{2}&gt;\\frac{1+B(t)}2.\n\\]\nChoose positive rational \\(\\varepsilon,\\epsilon'\\) so small that\n\\[\n\\varepsilon&lt;\\frac{t-B(t)}4\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\alpha\\left(\\frac{1+t}2-\\varepsilon-\\epsilon'\\right)\n&gt;\n\\frac{1+B(t)}2+\\varepsilon+\\epsilon'.\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]</p><p>The required weighted gap follows directly from the preceding propositions. Set \\(\\delta=\\varepsilon\\), choose rational \\(0&lt;\\mu&lt;1/2\\) with \\(t\\mu\\le\\varepsilon/4\\), and use Proposition 10 to fix\n\\[\ns,m,p_*,\\theta.\n\\]\nChoose rational\n\\[\n0&lt;b&lt;2^{-2s}p_*\\theta/2.\n\\]\nProposition 2 fixes \\(n=n(b)\\) before completeness is chosen. Choose\n\\[\nT\\ge\\frac{2^{n(2k-2s)}}{(p_*\\theta)^2},\n\\]\nand then positive rational \\(a&lt;1-b\\) with \\(2Ta\\le\\varepsilon/4\\).</p><p>Apply the NP-hard game gap of Proposition 2 and construct the graph of Proposition 4. In the YES case it has cut value at least\n\\[\n\\frac{1+t}2-\\varepsilon/2.\n\\]\nIn the NO case, a cut of value at least\n\\[\n\\frac{1+B(t)}2+\\varepsilon/2\n\\]\nwould, by Proposition 5, give expected odd slice stability at least \\(B(t)+\\varepsilon\\). Propositions 10 and 14 would imply\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\n\\ge2^{-2s}p_*\\theta/2&gt;b,\n\\]\na contradiction. Thus this deterministic polynomial construction supplies, in particular, the weaker gap\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Val}\\ge(1+t)/2-\\varepsilon\n\\quad\\text{versus}\\quad\n\\operatorname{Val}\\le(1+B(t))/2+\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p><p>Delete loops without changing the original weight scale; remaining weights sum to at most one. Apply Proposition 15 with error \\(\\epsilon'\\), producing a simple unweighted graph whose maximum cut, on scale \\(Q\\), differs from the original maximum cut by at most \\(\\epsilon'\\).</p><p>An \\(\\alpha\\)-approximation on this graph returns, in the YES case, a cut of scaled value at least the left side of (9.2). In the NO case, no cut has scaled value greater than its right side. A fixed rational threshold strictly between them distinguishes the NP-hard promise cases. This proves the theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "It is NP-hard to distinguish \\( \\mathrm{YES}:\\quad \\operatorname{Val}(G)\\ge \\frac{1+t}{2}-\\varepsilon \\) from \\( \\mathrm{NO}:\\quad \\operatorname{Val}(G)\\le \\frac{1+B(t)}2+\\varepsilon. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A weighted graph is given explicitly by a rational probability distribution on unordered edges; loops are allowed and contribute zero to every cut.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\operatorname{Val}(G)\\) for its maximum cut weight.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  B(t)=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\arcsin t,\\qquad 0&lt;t&lt;1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a rational \\(t\\in(0,1)\\) and a constant\n  \\[\n  0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;\\frac{t-B(t)}4.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>It is NP-hard to distinguish\n\\[\n\\mathrm{YES}:\\quad\n\\operatorname{Val}(G)\\ge \\frac{1+t}{2}-\\varepsilon\n\\]\nfrom\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NO}:\\quad\n\\operatorname{Val}(G)\\le \\frac{1+B(t)}2+\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix rational \\(t\\) and \\(\\varepsilon\\) as in the theorem. Put \\(\\delta=\\varepsilon\\). The condition\n\\[\n0&lt;\\delta&lt;1-B(t)\n\\]\nholds because\n\\[\n\\varepsilon&lt;\\frac{t-B(t)}4&lt;\\frac{1-B(t)}4.\n\\]\nChoose rational \\(0&lt;\\mu&lt;1/2\\) with \\(t\\mu\\le\\varepsilon/4\\). Apply Proposition 10 with \\(t,\\delta,\\mu\\), fixing\n\\[\ns,m,p_*,\\theta,\n\\]\nand hence \\(k,d,r\\).</p><p>Choose rational\n\\[\n0&lt;b&lt;2^{-2s}\\frac{p_*\\theta}{2}.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 2, this fixes an alphabet dimension \\(n\\) for which the completeness error may subsequently be made arbitrarily small. Choose an integer\n\\[\nT\\ge\n\\frac{2^{n(2k-2s)}}{(p_*\\theta)^2}.\n\\]\nFinally choose positive rational \\(a&lt;1-b\\) so small that\n\\[\n2Ta\\le\\varepsilon/4.\n\\]\nThe parameter order is\n\\[\n(t,\\varepsilon,\\mu)\n\\longrightarrow\n(s,m,p_*,\\theta)\n\\longrightarrow b\n\\longrightarrow n\n\\longrightarrow T\n\\longrightarrow a.\n\\]</p><p>Start from the NP-hard game gap\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\\ge1-a\n\\quad\\text{versus}\\quad\n\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\\le b.\n\\]\nConstruct the graph of Proposition 4.</p><p>In the YES case, Proposition 4 gives cut value at least\n\\[\n\\frac{1+t}2-t\\mu-2Ta\n\\ge\n\\frac{1+t}2-\\varepsilon/2,\n\\]\nwhich is stronger than required.</p><p>In the NO case, suppose a cut had value at least\n\\[\n\\frac{1+B(t)}2+\\varepsilon/2.\n\\]\nProposition 5 gives expected odd slice stability at least \\(B(t)+\\varepsilon\\). Propositions 10 and 14 then imply\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\n\\ge2^{-2s}p_*\\theta/2&gt;b,\n\\]\ncontradicting the NO promise. Every cut therefore has value less than\n\\[\n\\frac{1+B(t)}2+\\varepsilon/2,\n\\]\nagain stronger than required.</p><p>For fixed \\(t,\\varepsilon\\), every chosen parameter is constant relative to the input size. Proposition 4’s enumeration gives a deterministic polynomial-size graph with rational edge probabilities of polynomial bit complexity. It satisfies the asserted weaker \\(\\varepsilon\\)-loss bounds, proving the theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, Majority Is Stablest with a degree cutoff", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are an integer \\(K\\ge1\\) and \\(\\tau>0\\), depending only on \\(t,\\xi\\), such that every \\(h:\\{-1,1\\}^{\\Omega}\\to[-1,1]\\) with \\(\\mathbb Eh=0\\) satisfies …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega\\) be a finite set, with the uniform probability measure on \\(\\{-1,1\\}^{\\Omega}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(h:\\{-1,1\\}^{\\Omega}\\to\\mathbb R\\), use the probability-normalized expansion\n  \\[\n  h(x)=\\sum_{S\\subseteq\\Omega}\\widehat h(S)\\prod_{a\\in S}x_a.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Inf}_a(h)=\\sum_{S\\ni a}\\widehat h(S)^2,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{Inf}^{\\le K}_a(h)\n  =\n  \\sum_{\\substack{S\\ni a\\\\|S|\\le K}}\\widehat h(S)^2.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(P_{\\le K}\\) denote projection onto Fourier degree at most \\(K\\). Then\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{a\\in\\Omega}\\operatorname{Inf}_a(P_{\\le K}h)\n  \\le K\\|h\\|_2^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For independently coupled \\(t\\)-correlated uniform coordinate pairs,\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Stab}_t(h)\n  =\n  \\mathbb E[h(x)h(y)]\n  =\n  \\sum_{S\\subseteq\\Omega}t^{|S|}\\widehat h(S)^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\) and \\(\\xi&gt;0\\), and put \\(B(t)=2\\arcsin(t)/\\pi\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are an integer \\(K\\ge1\\) and \\(\\tau&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(t,\\xi\\), such that every \\(h:\\{-1,1\\}^{\\Omega}\\to[-1,1]\\) with \\(\\mathbb Eh=0\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\operatorname{Inf}^{\\le K}_a(h)&lt;\\tau\n\\text{ for all }a\\in\\Omega\n\\right)\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Stab}_t(h)\\le B(t)+\\xi.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The untruncated assertion follows from Majority Is Stablest [16, Theorem 4.4]. To match its \\([0,1]\\) convention, put \\(g=(h+1)/2\\). Then \\(\\mathbb Eg=1/2\\), each nonconstant Fourier coefficient is halved, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Stab}_t(h)=4\\operatorname{Stab}_t(g)-1.\n\\]\nThe Gaussian halfspace value for \\(g\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac14+\\frac{\\arcsin(t)}{2\\pi},\n\\]\nwhich becomes \\(B(t)\\) in this normalization; see [16, Theorem 4.1 and Corollary 4.3] and Borell [5].</p><p>For the degree-cutoff reduction, let \\(T_u\\) be ordinary bit noise with parameter \\(u&lt;1\\), and put \\(h_u=T_uh\\). Range and mean are preserved. Uniformly over \\(\\|h\\|_2\\le1\\),\n\\[\n0\\le\n\\operatorname{Stab}_t(h)-\\operatorname{Stab}_t(h_u)\n\\le\n\\sup_{j\\ge0}t^j(1-u^{2j})\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(u\\uparrow1).\n\\]\nThe convergence follows by first choosing a finite degree cutoff to bound the tail \\(t^j\\), and then taking \\(u\\) close to one on the remaining degrees.</p><p>Choose \\(u\\) so that this error is at most \\(\\xi/2\\). Let \\(\\delta_0&gt;0\\) be the untruncated influence threshold for error \\(\\xi/2\\). Choose \\(K\\) with\n\\[\nu^{2(K+1)}\\le\\delta_0/2,\n\\]\nand take \\(\\tau=\\delta_0/2\\). Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inf}_a(h_u)\n\\le\n\\operatorname{Inf}^{\\le K}_a(h)\n+\nu^{2(K+1)}\\|h\\|_2^2\n&lt;\n\\delta_0.\n\\]\nApply the untruncated theorem to \\(h_u\\) and add the stability error.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 2.2, Affine 2-to-1 Games Theorem", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every constant \\(b\\in(0,1)\\), there is an integer \\(n=n(b)\\ge1\\) such that, for every constant \\(a\\in(0,1-b)\\), the following promise problem is NP-hard.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A weighted bipartite projection game consists of finite vertex sets \\(X,Y\\), a rational probability distribution \\(\\lambda\\) on a finite edge set \\(E\\), endpoint maps to \\(X,Y\\), finite label sets, and a projection \\(\\pi_e\\) for each edge. Parallel edges are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Its value is\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\n  =\n  \\max_{\\ell_X,\\ell_Y}\n  \\Pr_{e=(x,y)\\sim\\lambda}\n  [\\,\\pi_e(\\ell_X(x))=\\ell_Y(y)\\,].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The external construction is Dinur–Khot–Kindler–Minzer–Safra [6, Sections 4–5]. Its Grassmann agreement hypothesis is supplied by Khot–Minzer–Safra [14] and the expansion-to-agreement implications of Barak–Kothari–Steurer [3, Theorems 9–11].</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every constant \\(b\\in(0,1)\\), there is an integer \\(n=n(b)\\ge1\\) such that, for every constant \\(a\\in(0,1-b)\\), the following promise problem is NP-hard.</p><p>The input is a weighted bipartite game with respective label sets\n\\[\n\\mathbb F_2^{n+1},\\qquad \\mathbb F_2^n\n\\]\nand a surjective affine map\n\\[\n\\pi_e:\\mathbb F_2^{n+1}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb F_2^n\n\\]\non every edge. The promise cases are\n\\[\n\\mathrm{YES}:\\quad\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\\ge1-a,\n\\qquad\n\\mathrm{NO}:\\quad\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\\le b.\n\\]\nThe integer \\(n\\) depends on \\(b\\) alone. For fixed \\(a,b\\), the reduction is deterministic and has polynomial running time and output size.</p>", "proof": "<p>Given \\(b\\in(0,1)\\), apply Lemma 2.4 with\n\\[\n\\delta=b/4.\n\\]\nAll restrictions in that lemma depend only on \\(b\\) and parameters fixed from it. In particular, the alphabet dimension\n\\[\nn=\\ell-1\n\\]\nis fixed using only \\(b\\).</p><p>Retain the valid raw weights of Lemma 2.1. Their total mass satisfies \\(q_{\\mathrm{edge}}\\ge1/2\\), so the normalized game in the soundness case has\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\n\\le\n\\delta/q_{\\mathrm{edge}}\n\\le b/2&lt;b.\n\\]\nThe affine maps and two-element fibers are supplied by Lemma 2.1.</p><p>After \\(\\ell,k_0\\) have been fixed for soundness, choose the initial \\(3\\mathrm{Lin}\\) completeness error sufficiently small that\n\\[\n\\epsilon_0\\le a/(2k_0).\n\\]\nLemma 2.5 gives completeness at least \\(1-a\\), without changing \\(n\\), and supplies deterministic polynomial effectivity and size. This establishes the quantifier order\n\\[\n\\forall b\\in(0,1)\\ \\exists n=n(b)\\ge1\\\n\\forall a\\in(0,1-b).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Appendix A.1, folded game, affine projections, and valid-subspace conditioning", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The valid folded constraints are surjective affine maps from an \\(\\ell\\)-dimensional binary answer space to an \\((\\ell-1)\\)-dimensional binary answer space, each with two-element fibers.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Theorem 4.1 of [6], obtained from Håstad’s theorem [10], supplies an absolute \\(s_*&lt;1\\) such that, for every sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon_0&gt;0\\), it is NP-hard to distinguish regular binary \\(3\\mathrm{Lin}\\) instances of value at least \\(1-\\epsilon_0\\) from those of value at most \\(s_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Regularity includes bounded variable occurrence, three distinct variables per equation, and at most one variable shared by two distinct equations.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix integers \\(\\ell\\ge2\\), \\(k_0\\ge\\ell\\), and a rational \\(\\beta\\in(0,1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) be the variables, \\(V_0=\\mathbb F_2^Z\\), and write each equation \\(e\\) as\n  \\[\n  \\langle x_e,\\alpha\\rangle=b_e,\n  \\]\n  where \\(x_e\\) has its three variable coordinates equal to one.</p><p class=\"li\">A legitimate tuple \\(U\\) consists of \\(k_0\\) disjoint equations, with the additional condition that variables from different equations of \\(U\\) do not occur together in any input equation. Set\n  \\[\n  V_U=\\mathbb F_2^{\\operatorname{var}(U)},\\qquad\n  H_U=\\operatorname{span}\\{x_e:e\\in U\\}.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(h_U:H_U\\to\\mathbb F_2\\) be the unique linear function satisfying \\(h_U(x_e)=b_e\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An unfolded left question is \\((U,L)\\), where\n  \\[\n  L\\le V_U,\\qquad \\dim L=\\ell,\\qquad L\\cap H_U=\\{0\\}.\n  \\]\n  For a variable set \\(V\\), put \\(V_V=\\mathbb F_2^V\\). A right question is \\((V,L')\\), where \\(L'\\le V_V\\) and \\(\\dim L'=\\ell-1\\). On an incident pair \\(L'\\le L\\), the unfolded constraint restricts a linear form on \\(L\\) to \\(L'\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Folding identifies \\((U,L)\\) and \\((U_1,L_1)\\) when\n  \\[\n  L+H_U+H_{U_1}=L_1+H_U+H_{U_1}.\n  \\]\n  By [6, Lemma 4.2], this is an equivalence relation, and every class \\(C\\) has an \\(\\ell\\)-space \\(R_C\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  R_C+H_U=L+H_U\n  \\qquad((U,L)\\in C).\n  \\tag{A.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The edge sampler first samples \\(k_0\\) equations independently. For legitimate \\(U\\), independently for each equation, \\(V\\) contains all three variables with probability \\(1-\\beta\\), and otherwise one uniformly chosen variable. It then samples a uniform \\((\\ell-1)\\)-space \\(L'\\le V_V\\), and a uniform \\(\\ell\\)-space \\(L\\le V_U\\) containing \\(L'\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The valid folded constraints are surjective affine maps from an \\(\\ell\\)-dimensional binary answer space to an \\((\\ell-1)\\)-dimensional binary answer space, each with two-element fibers.</p><p>The sampler can be conditioned on legitimacy and \\(L\\cap H_U=\\{0\\}\\). Its discarded probability is at most\n\\[\n\\kappa=\nO(k_0^2/|Z|)\n+\n2^{\\ell-k_0}\n+\n2^{\\ell-2k_0}.\n\\tag{A.3}\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\ell,k_0\\), disjoint copies of the input can make the first term arbitrarily small without changing its value or regularity.</p><p>Let \\(P\\) be the sampler conditioned only on legitimacy, let\n\\[\nE_{\\mathrm{valid}}=\\{L\\cap H_U=\\{0\\}\\},\n\\qquad\n\\eta_{\\mathrm{valid}}=2^{\\ell-k_0}+2^{\\ell-2k_0},\n\\]\nand let \\(R=P(\\,\\cdot\\mid E_{\\mathrm{valid}})\\). Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}(P,R)\\le\\eta_{\\mathrm{valid}}.\n\\]\nWriting \\(w(e)\\) for the raw valid edge weights and\n\\[\nq_{\\mathrm{edge}}=\\sum_e w(e)=P(E_{\\mathrm{valid}}),\n\\]\none has \\(q_{\\mathrm{edge}}\\ge1-\\eta_{\\mathrm{valid}}\\) and \\(R(e)=w(e)/q_{\\mathrm{edge}}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The vectors \\(x_e\\), \\(e\\in U\\), are independent, so \\(\\dim V_U=3k_0\\), \\(\\dim H_U=k_0\\), and \\(h_U\\) is well defined.</p><p>Both sides of (A.1) have dimension \\(\\ell+k_0\\); consequently \\(R_C\\cap H_U=\\{0\\}\\). A folded answer is a linear form \\(\\sigma:R_C\\to\\mathbb F_2\\). Its unfolding on \\((U,L)\\) is the restriction to \\(L\\) of\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\sigma_U(r+h)=\\sigma(r)+h_U(h),\n\\qquad r\\in R_C,\\ h\\in H_U.\n\\tag{A.2}\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\in L\\), write uniquely\n\\[\nz=A_Uz+B_Uz,\n\\qquad\nA_Uz\\in R_C,\\quad B_Uz\\in H_U.\n\\]\nThe map \\(A_U:L\\to R_C\\) is a linear isomorphism by (A.1). Thus unfolding is the affine bijection\n\\[\n\\sigma\\longmapsto \\sigma\\circ A_U+h_U\\circ B_U\n\\]\nfrom \\(R_C^*\\) to \\(L^*\\). Composing it with the surjective restriction \\(L^*\\to(L')^*\\) gives a surjective affine map whose linear part has one-dimensional kernel. Every fiber has exactly two elements. Choosing bases identifies the answer spaces with \\(\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\) and \\(\\mathbb F_2^{\\ell-1}\\). Claim 4.4 of [6] shows that projections agree when unfolded edges are aggregated into the same folded edge; alternatively, they may be kept separately with their individual weights.</p><p>Sampling \\(k_0\\) equations independently produces a nonlegitimate tuple with probability \\(O(k_0^2/|Z|)\\), by bounded occurrence.</p><p>If \\(f_0\\) equations contribute all three variables to \\(V\\), then\n\\[\n\\dim V_V=k_0+2f_0,\\qquad\n\\dim(V_V\\cap H_U)=f_0.\n\\]\nCounting nonzero vectors in a uniform \\((\\ell-1)\\)-space gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[L'\\cap H_U\\ne\\{0\\}]\n\\le\n\\frac{(2^{f_0}-1)(2^{\\ell-1}-1)}\n     {2^{k_0+2f_0}-1}\n\\le 2^{\\ell-k_0}.\n\\]\nIf \\(L'\\cap H_U=\\{0\\}\\), a uniform \\(\\ell\\)-space containing \\(L'\\) is obtained by choosing a nonzero vector of \\(V_U/L'\\). It intersects \\(H_U\\) nontrivially precisely when this vector belongs to \\((H_U+L')/L'\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\Pr[L\\cap H_U\\ne\\{0\\}\\mid L']\n=\n\\frac{2^{k_0}-1}{2^{3k_0-\\ell+1}-1}\n\\le2^{\\ell-2k_0}.\n\\]\nThis proves (A.3).</p><p>Increase \\(k_0\\) after fixing \\(\\ell\\) so that the two geometric terms sum to at most \\(1/4\\). By taking disjoint copies if necessary, make nonlegitimacy have probability at most \\(1/4\\). The total retained probability \\(q_{\\mathrm{all}}\\), before either conditioning step, is therefore at least \\(1/2\\).</p><p>Conditioned on legitimacy,\n\\[\nP(E_{\\mathrm{valid}}^c)\\le\\eta_{\\mathrm{valid}},\n\\]\nso \\(\\operatorname{TV}(P,R)=P(E_{\\mathrm{valid}}^c)\\). On valid folded edges,\n\\[\nw(e)=\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\omega\\in E_{\\mathrm{valid}}\\\\\\omega\\mapsto e}}P(\\omega),\n\\qquad\nq_{\\mathrm{edge}}=P(E_{\\mathrm{valid}}).\n\\]\nNormalization gives the asserted edge law. A folded labeling’s acceptance under \\(R\\) is its raw valid acceptance divided by \\(q_{\\mathrm{edge}}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Appendix A.2, completion of the folded tables", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One has \\( H_U\\cap V_V=H(V). \\) There are well-defined total tables \\(F_V\\) on the \\(\\ell\\)-spaces of \\(V_V\\), obtained from any containing legitimate \\(U\\) on valid spaces and set to zero on invalid spaces, such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the regular \\(3\\mathrm{Lin}\\) instance, legitimate tuples, spaces \\(V_U,H_U\\), forms \\(h_U\\), and folded game of Lemma 2.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a folded labeling.</p><p class=\"li\">For every right variable set \\(V\\) occurring with a legitimate tuple, define\n  \\[\n  H(V)=\n  \\operatorname{span}\n  \\{x_e:e\\text{ is an input equation and }\\operatorname{var}(e)\\subseteq V\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(F_U[L]\\), for every \\(\\ell\\)-space \\(L\\le V_U\\), to be the unfolded answer when \\(L\\cap H_U=\\{0\\}\\), and the zero linear form otherwise.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\nH_U\\cap V_V=H(V).\n\\tag{A.4}\n\\]\nThere are well-defined total tables \\(F_V\\) on the \\(\\ell\\)-spaces of \\(V_V\\), obtained from any containing legitimate \\(U\\) on valid spaces and set to zero on invalid spaces, such that\n\\[\nF_U[L]=F_V[L]\n\\qquad(L\\le V_V,\\ \\dim L=\\ell).\n\\tag{A.5}\n\\]\nMoreover, for every \\((\\ell+k_0)\\)-space \\(K\\supseteq H_U\\), there is a linear form \\(f_K:K\\to\\mathbb F_2\\) with\n\\[\nf_K|_{H_U}=h_U,\\qquad\nF_U[L]=f_K|_L\n\\quad\\text{whenever }L+H_U=K.\n\\]\nThe auxiliary table \\(F_V\\) is on \\(\\ell\\)-spaces and is distinct from the game’s right-answer table on \\((\\ell-1)\\)-spaces.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every input equation supported in \\(\\operatorname{var}(U)\\) belongs to \\(U\\): an equation using two tuple blocks violates legitimacy, and a distinct equation inside one block would share three variables with that block’s equation. Since the equations of \\(U\\) have disjoint supports, this proves (A.4).</p><p>In particular, for \\(L\\subseteq V_V\\), validity of \\((U,L)\\) is independent of the containing legitimate tuple \\(U\\). For valid \\(L\\), all vertices \\((U,L)\\) with that same \\(L\\) lie in one folding class; their unfolded answers agree by [6, Claim 4.4]. Equation (A.4) gives the same independence for invalid \\(L\\), which are all assigned zero. Thus the total tables \\(F_V\\) are well defined and satisfy (A.5).</p><p>Fix \\(K\\supseteq H_U\\) of dimension \\(\\ell+k_0\\). All valid \\(L\\) with \\(L+H_U=K\\) belong to one folding class. The common folded label and its \\((H_U,h_U)\\)-extension give one linear form \\(f_K\\) on \\(K\\) with the stated properties. This condition imposes no restriction on invalid entries.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma A.1, invariant-list assertion on admissible advice spaces", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every maximal occurring pair satisfies \\( H_0\\subseteq W,\\qquad g|_{H_0}=h_0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_0\\) have dimension \\(3k_0\\), let \\(H_0\\le X_0\\) have dimension \\(k_0\\), and let \\(h_0:H_0\\to\\mathbb F_2\\) be linear.</p><p class=\"li\">A table \\(F\\) assigns a linear form to every \\(\\ell\\)-space. For every \\((\\ell+k_0)\\)-space \\(K\\supseteq H_0\\), suppose there is a linear form \\(f_K\\) with\n  \\[\n  f_K|_{H_0}=h_0,\\qquad F[L]=f_K|_L\n  \\quad\\text{whenever }L+H_0=K.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a \\(q\\)-space \\(Q\\) with \\(Q\\cap H_0=\\{0\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;\\tau_0&lt;\\cdots&lt;\\tau_r\\le1/2\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\tau_i=10^{-9}\\tau_{i+1}^{12}\\qquad(0\\le i&lt;r).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  e_{k_0}=2^{\\ell+r-2k_0+1},\n  \\]\n  and assume\n  \\[\n  q\\le\\ell,\\qquad 3k_0-r&gt;\\ell,\\qquad\n  2^{q-\\ell}\\le\\tau_0/4,\\qquad e_{k_0}\\le\\tau_0/4.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(Q\\subseteq W\\subseteq X_0\\) of codimension \\(i\\le r\\), a pair \\((g,W)\\), with \\(g:W\\to\\mathbb F_2\\) linear, occurs if\n  \\[\n  \\Pr_{\\substack{Q\\subseteq L\\subseteq W\\\\\\dim L=\\ell}}\n  [F[L]=g|_L]&gt;\\tau_i.\n  \\]\n  It is maximal if it has no occurring extension to a strictly larger space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every maximal occurring pair satisfies\n\\[\nH_0\\subseteq W,\\qquad g|_{H_0}=h_0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(H'=H_0\\cap W\\). In \\(W/Q\\), the image \\((H'+Q)/Q\\) has dimension \\(\\dim H'\\), since \\(Q\\cap H_0=\\{0\\}\\). Counting nonzero vectors in a uniform \\((\\ell-q)\\)-space gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_W\n&amp;:=\n\\Pr_{\\substack{Q\\subseteq L\\subseteq W\\\\\\dim L=\\ell}}\n[L\\cap H_0\\ne\\{0\\}]\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac{(2^{\\dim H'}-1)(2^{\\ell-q}-1)}\n     {2^{\\dim W-q}-1}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n2^{\\ell+\\dim H'-\\dim W+1}\n\\le e_{k_0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe same bound holds with \\(W\\) replaced by any containing space of codimension at most \\(r\\).</p><p>Partition valid \\(L\\), with \\(Q\\subseteq L\\subseteq W\\), by \\(Y=L+H'\\). Within a fixed class, the table is the restriction of one linear form on \\(Y\\) respecting \\(h_0|_{H'}\\); this follows by restricting \\(f_{L+H_0}\\).</p><p>If \\(g|_{H'}\\ne h_0|_{H'}\\), the difference of these two forms is nonzero on \\(H'\\). A complement to \\(H'\\) in \\(Y\\) containing \\(Q\\) is a graph of a linear map on an \\((\\ell-q)\\)-dimensional quotient. Each of its \\(\\ell-q\\) independent lift choices must satisfy one nontrivial linear equation for the two forms to agree. Agreement within this class is therefore at most \\(2^{q-\\ell}\\), and is zero if the forms already disagree on \\(Q\\). Total agreement is at most\n\\[\nd_W+(1-d_W)2^{q-\\ell}\n\\le e_{k_0}+2^{q-\\ell}\n\\le\\tau_0/2&lt;\\tau_i,\n\\]\na contradiction. Thus \\(g|_{H'}=h_0|_{H'}\\).</p><p>There is consequently a unique extension \\(\\widetilde g\\) to\n\\[\n\\widetilde W=W+H_0\n\\]\nrespecting \\(h_0\\). Under either uniform distribution on valid \\(\\ell\\)-spaces containing \\(Q\\), in \\(W\\) or in \\(\\widetilde W\\), the image of \\(L\\) in\n\\[\nW/(W\\cap H_0)\\simeq\\widetilde W/H_0\n\\]\nis uniform among the \\(\\ell\\)-spaces containing the image of \\(Q\\). Every such image has the same number of complements containing \\(Q\\), namely\n\\[\n2^{(\\ell-q)\\dim(H_0\\cap W)}\n\\quad\\text{in }W,\n\\qquad\n2^{(\\ell-q)\\dim H_0}\n\\quad\\text{in }\\widetilde W.\n\\]\nAgreement depends only on this image: \\(f_{L+H_0}\\) and \\(\\widetilde g\\) agree on \\(H_0\\), so their difference vanishes on one complement precisely when it vanishes on every complement. Conditional agreement on valid spaces is therefore identical in the two ambient spaces.</p><p>Since agreement in \\(W\\) is at least \\(\\tau_i\\), agreement in \\(\\widetilde W\\) is at least\n\\[\n(1-d_{\\widetilde W})\\frac{\\tau_i-d_W}{1-d_W}\n\\ge\n(1-e_{k_0})(\\tau_i-e_{k_0})\n\\ge\n\\tau_i-2e_{k_0}\n\\ge\\tau_i/2.\n\\]\nIf \\(H_0\\not\\subseteq W\\), the codimension \\(j\\) of \\(\\widetilde W\\) is strictly smaller than \\(i\\), and\n\\[\n\\tau_j\\le\\tau_{i-1}&lt;\\tau_i/2.\n\\]\nThis gives an occurring extension, contradicting maximality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Appendix A.2–A.3, soundness of the completed-table construction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The parameters \\(\\ell,k_0,\\beta\\) can be chosen using only \\(\\delta\\) and \\(s_*\\), with \\(\\ell\\) fixed before \\(k_0,\\beta\\), so that every folded labeling has raw valid acceptance at most \\(\\delta\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the folded game of Lemma 2.1 and completed tables of Lemma 2.2, starting from a regular binary \\(3\\mathrm{Lin}\\) instance of value at most \\(s_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Raw valid acceptance means acceptance under the law conditioned on legitimate \\(U\\), with payoff zero when \\(L\\cap H_U\\ne\\{0\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a total table \\(F\\), \\(\\operatorname{agreement}(F)\\) is the acceptance probability of the test choosing a uniform \\((\\ell-1)\\)-space, then two independent uniform \\(\\ell\\)-spaces containing it, and comparing their restrictions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a soundness parameter \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The parameters \\(\\ell,k_0,\\beta\\) can be chosen using only \\(\\delta\\) and \\(s_*\\), with \\(\\ell\\) fixed before \\(k_0,\\beta\\), so that every folded labeling has raw valid acceptance at most \\(\\delta\\). These choices do not depend on the completeness error of the initial \\(3\\mathrm{Lin}\\) instance.</p>", "proof": "<p>The agreement lemma [6, Lemma 3.7] supplies constants \\(q,r,C\\) and a positive function \\(\\alpha(\\ell)\\) at agreement threshold \\(\\delta^2/8\\), as in the proof of [6, Lemma 5.3]. Set\n\\[\n\\tau_r=\\min\\{C/2,1/2\\},\\qquad\n\\tau_i=10^{-9}\\tau_{i+1}^{12}\\quad(0\\le i&lt;r).\n\\]\nAlso set\n\\[\n\\zeta_r=\\tau_0/2,\\qquad\n\\zeta_i=10^{-9}\\zeta_{i+1}^{12}\\quad(0\\le i&lt;r).\n\\]\nBoth threshold vectors are fixed before choosing \\(\\ell\\). Choose \\(\\ell\\) large enough for the agreement lemma and the ordinary list bound [6, Lemma 3.12] on \\((\\ell-q)\\)-spaces with thresholds \\((\\zeta_i)\\). Require\n\\[\nq\\le\\ell-1,\\qquad 2^{q-\\ell}\\le\\tau_0/4.\n\\tag{A.6}\n\\]\nChoose \\(k_0\\) subsequently, large enough for the ambient-dimension requirements in every dimension between \\(k_0\\) and \\(3k_0\\), and in their quotients by a \\(q\\)-space.</p><p>For a fixed \\(q\\)-space \\(Q\\), the \\(\\tau\\)-list records maximal occurring pairs on spaces of codimension at most \\(r\\), as in Lemma 2.3. Applying that lemma with\n\\[\nX_0=V_U,\\qquad H_0=H_U,\\qquad h_0=h_U\n\\]\nshows that every maximal pair decoded from \\(F_U\\) respects every equation of \\(U\\), provided \\(Q\\cap H_U=\\{0\\}\\).</p><p>The outer game of [6, Section 5.2] samples \\((U,V)\\), then a uniform \\(q\\)-space \\(Q\\le V_V\\). It sends \\((U,Q)\\) and \\((V,Q)\\) to its players, asks for assignments on \\(\\operatorname{var}(U)\\) and \\(V\\), and accepts when the assignments agree on \\(V\\) and the first satisfies every equation of \\(U\\).</p><p>If \\(f_0\\) equations contributed all three variables, then\n\\[\n\\dim V_V=k_0+2f_0,\\qquad \\dim H(V)=f_0.\n\\]\nUniformly in \\(U,V\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr[Q\\cap H_U\\ne\\{0\\}\\mid U,V]\n\\le\n\\frac{(2^{f_0}-1)(2^q-1)}\n     {2^{k_0+2f_0}-1}\n\\le2^{q-k_0}.\n\\tag{A.7}\n\\]\nFurther increase \\(k_0\\) until\n\\[\ne_{k_0}=2^{\\ell+r-2k_0+1}\\le\\tau_0/4,\n\\qquad\n2^{q-k_0}\\le\\delta\\alpha(\\ell)/8.\n\\tag{A.8}\n\\]</p><p>Suppose a folded labeling has raw valid acceptance greater than \\(\\delta\\). Testing the completed \\(F_U[L]\\) against the original answer on \\((V,L')\\) can only increase this acceptance: valid entries are unchanged, and invalid outcomes previously had payoff zero. The agreement/list and covering arguments in [6, Lemma 5.3, Claim 5.6, and Equation (5.1)] therefore apply. Before invoking invariance, those arguments use total linear tables, their agreement, and (A.5), all of which are now defined.</p><p>For fixed \\(U,Q\\), let \\(\\mathcal L_{U,Q}\\) be the uniform law on \\(\\ell\\)-spaces of \\(V_U\\) containing \\(Q\\). Let \\(\\mathcal L'_{U,Q}\\) first sample \\(V\\) conditional on \\((U,Q)\\) in the outer game and then a uniform \\(\\ell\\)-space of \\(V_V\\) containing \\(Q\\). These are the conditional laws in [6, Lemma 4.11]. Indeed, if \\(N_q(d)\\) counts \\(q\\)-spaces in \\(\\mathbb F_2^d\\), then\n\\[\n\\Pr[Q\\subseteq L\\mid V]\n=\n\\frac{N_q(\\ell)}{N_q(\\dim V_V)}.\n\\]\nThus conditioning the source’s \\(V,L\\) sampler on \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) weights \\(V\\) proportionally to\n\\[\n\\Pr[V\\mid U]/N_q(\\dim V_V),\n\\]\nexactly as in the outer game.</p><p>Let \\(E_1\\) be\n\\[\n\\operatorname{agreement}(F_U)&gt;\\delta^2/8,\n\\]\nlet \\(E_2\\) be\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}(\\mathcal L_{U,Q},\\mathcal L'_{U,Q})\n\\le2^{\\ell+5}\\sqrt\\beta\\,k_0^{1/4},\n\\]\nand let \\(E_3\\) be nonemptiness of the \\(Q\\)-list of \\(F_U\\) at thresholds \\((\\tau_i)\\). Event \\(E_2\\) is the smoothness condition in [6, Equation (4.3)]. For \\(E_3\\), [6, Lemma 3.7] supplies an occurring pair for an \\(\\alpha(\\ell)\\) fraction of uniform \\(Q\\) whenever \\(E_1\\) holds; any such pair has a maximal occurring extension. Only existence, not a list-size bound, is used here.</p><p>The cited arguments give\n\\[\n\\Pr[E_1\\cap E_2\\cap E_3]\\ge\\delta\\alpha(\\ell)/4.\n\\]\nIntersecting with\n\\[\nE_4=\\{Q\\cap H_U=\\{0\\}\\}\n\\]\nleaves probability at least \\(\\delta\\alpha(\\ell)/8\\), by (A.7)–(A.8). Since \\(E_4\\) depends only on \\(U,Q\\), it does not alter the subsequent conditional law of \\(V\\) once \\(U,Q\\) are fixed.</p><p>For the second player, fix a question \\((V,Q)\\). For each linear form \\(\\gamma:Q\\to\\mathbb F_2\\), fix an extension \\(p_\\gamma:V_V\\to\\mathbb F_2\\), and let\n\\[\n\\nu:V_V\\to V_V/Q\n\\]\nbe the quotient map. Define a total table on \\((\\ell-q)\\)-spaces by\n\\[\nT_\\gamma[L/Q]\n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\overline{F_V[L]-p_\\gamma|_L},\n&amp;F_V[L]|_Q=\\gamma,\\\\\n0,&amp;F_V[L]|_Q\\ne\\gamma.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe overline is the induced linear form on \\(L/Q\\), defined because the difference vanishes on \\(Q\\).</p><p>Use the ordinary maximal list of \\(T_\\gamma\\) at thresholds \\((\\zeta_i)\\). By [6, Lemma 3.12], its size is at most\n\\[\nM_0=M_0(\\ell-q,r,\\zeta_r),\n\\]\nindependent of \\(k_0\\). The second player chooses a uniform \\(\\gamma\\in Q^*\\), then a uniform maximal pair \\((g,S)\\) from that list. It lifts \\(g\\) to\n\\[\ng\\circ\\nu+p_\\gamma\n\\quad\\text{on }\\nu^{-1}(S),\n\\]\nextends uniformly to \\(V_V\\), and outputs the corresponding assignment. If the list is empty, it uses an arbitrary assignment. The procedure uses only \\((V,Q)\\) and the fixed tables.</p><p>The first player chooses a uniform maximal pair \\((f,W)\\) from the \\(\\tau\\)-list of \\(F_U\\) and extends \\(f\\) uniformly to \\(V_U\\), using an arbitrary assignment if the list is empty. This finite list need not have a size bound independent of \\(k_0\\), because the success estimate holds for every element.</p><p>Fix \\(U,Q\\in E_1\\cap E_2\\cap E_3\\cap E_4\\), and any first-player choice \\((f,W)\\), with\n\\[\ni=\\operatorname{codim}_{V_U}W\\le r.\n\\]\nLemma 2.3 gives\n\\[\nH_U\\subseteq W,\\qquad f|_{H_U}=h_U.\n\\]\nEvery extension chosen by the first player therefore satisfies all equations of \\(U\\).</p><p>The covering step of [6, Lemmas 4.11 and 5.5 and the proof of Lemma 5.3], using (A.5), gives probability at least \\(0.3\\tau_i\\ge0.3\\tau_0\\) over \\(V\\) that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{codim}_{V_V}(W\\cap V_V)=i,\n\\qquad\n\\Pr_{\\substack{Q\\subseteq L\\subseteq W\\cap V_V\\\\\\dim L=\\ell}}\n[F_V[L]=f|_L]&gt;\\tau_i/2.\n\\]\nSet \\(S_0=W\\cap V_V\\) and \\(\\gamma=f|_Q\\). Every equality in this display is retained in \\(T_\\gamma\\). Since\n\\[\n\\zeta_i\\le\\tau_0/2\\le\\tau_i/2,\n\\]\nthe quotient pair\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\overline{f|_{S_0}-p_\\gamma|_{S_0}},\nS_0/Q\n\\right)\n\\]\noccurs for \\(T_\\gamma\\), and has a maximal occurring extension. Lifting such an extension by the same \\(p_\\gamma\\) gives a form agreeing with \\(f\\) on all of \\(S_0\\). Extra agreements created by zero completion do not affect this function-extension conclusion.</p><p>The second player selects this tag and one such extension with probability at least \\(1/M\\), where\n\\[\nM=2^qM_0\n\\]\nis independent of \\(k_0\\). Its eventual answer agrees with \\(f\\) on \\(W\\cap V_V\\). The first player’s uniform extension agrees with that answer on all of \\(V_V\\) with probability\n\\[\n2^{-i}\\ge2^{-r}.\n\\]\nThus, conditionally on \\(E_1\\cap E_2\\cap E_3\\cap E_4\\), success probability is at least\n\\[\nc_*=2^{-r}\\frac{0.3\\tau_0}{M}&gt;0.\n\\]\nOverall success probability is at least\n\\[\n\\delta\\alpha(\\ell)c_*/8,\n\\]\na positive constant independent of \\(k_0\\).</p><p>The upper bound in [6, Lemma 5.4] tends to zero as \\(\\beta k_0\\to\\infty\\). After fixing \\(\\ell\\), take\n\\[\nk_0=j^4,\\qquad \\beta=j^{-3}\n\\]\nwith \\(j\\) sufficiently large. Then\n\\[\n\\beta k_0\\to\\infty,\\qquad\n\\beta\\sqrt{k_0}\\to0,\\qquad\n\\sqrt\\beta\\,k_0^{1/4}\\to0.\n\\]\nThese limits satisfy the outer-game soundness and covering requirements, including \\(2^\\ell\\beta\\le1/8\\), and all displayed margins. The resulting contradiction proves raw valid acceptance at most \\(\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Appendix A.3, completeness and effectivity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The conditioned game has value at least \\( 1-\\frac{k_0\\epsilon_0}{q_{\\mathrm{all}}} \\ge1-2k_0\\epsilon_0. \\) For fixed parameters, the folded game, its affine maps, and its rational weights are constructible …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the folded game of Lemma 2.1, with \\(\\ell,k_0,\\beta\\) fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose an assignment \\(\\alpha:Z\\to\\mathbb F_2\\) satisfies all but an \\(\\epsilon_0\\) fraction of the initial equations.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the total retained probability \\(q_{\\mathrm{all}}\\), before legitimacy and valid-subspace conditioning, is at least \\(1/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The conditioned game has value at least\n\\[\n1-\\frac{k_0\\epsilon_0}{q_{\\mathrm{all}}}\n\\ge1-2k_0\\epsilon_0.\n\\]\nFor fixed parameters, the folded game, its affine maps, and its rational weights are constructible deterministically in polynomial time and polynomial output size.</p>", "proof": "<p>Also write \\(\\alpha:V_0\\to\\mathbb F_2\\) for the linear extension of the assignment. Give folded question \\(C\\) the answer \\(\\alpha|_{R_C}\\), and give \\((V,L')\\) the answer \\(\\alpha|_{L'}\\). Whenever every equation of sampled \\(U\\) is satisfied,\n\\[\nh_U=\\alpha|_{H_U},\n\\]\nso (A.2) is exactly \\(\\alpha|_{R_C+H_U}\\). The sampled edge is then satisfied. This does not require every member of its folding class to involve satisfied equations.</p><p>Before either conditioning step, a union bound gives probability at most \\(k_0\\epsilon_0\\) of sampling an unsatisfied equation. Dividing by \\(q_{\\mathrm{all}}\\) proves the completeness bound.</p><p>All spaces in an individual question have bounded dimension. There are polynomially many \\(k_0\\)-tuples of input equations, and each has only constantly many subspaces, bases, and local sampling outcomes. The folding relation is explicitly testable by linear algebra.</p><p>To find \\(R_C\\), start from any \\((U,L)\\in C\\), enumerate the \\(\\ell\\)-spaces inside \\(L+H_U\\), whose dimension is the fixed constant \\(\\ell+k_0\\), and test each candidate against (A.1) for every class member. Lemma 4.2 of [6] guarantees a successful candidate. Gaussian elimination computes bases and affine maps. The finite sampling distributions, conditioning, and aggregation of parallel edges have rational weights of polynomial bit length.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, complementary traversal and correlation. The tree definition is Definition 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(F\\), the root output as a multilinear polynomial in the \\(k\\) leaf signs has only degree-\\(d\\) monomials.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a nonempty finite-dimensional affine space over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), with fixed affine coordinates, and put\n  \\[\n  H=\\operatorname{Aff}(A,\\mathbb F_2).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Evaluation identifies \\(A\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\{q\\in H^*:q(1)=1\\}.\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]\n  These functionals are called labels.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix integers \\(s\\ge2\\), \\(m\\ge1\\), and put\n  \\[\n  k=(2s)^m,\\qquad d=s^m.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal T\\) be the complete rooted \\(2s\\)-ary tree of depth \\(m\\), with children indexed by \\((g,b)\\in[s]\\times\\{0,1\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Each internal node \\(v\\) has a table \\(F_v:\\mathbb F_2^s\\to\\{-1,1\\}\\). Each leaf \\(i\\) has a row \\(R_i\\in H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The word \\(W(R,F)\\in\\{-1,1\\}^A\\) is evaluated at \\(a\\in A\\) as follows. Leaf \\(i\\) has sign \\((-1)^{R_i(a)}\\), and a gate with child signs \\(Z_{g,b}\\) outputs\n  \\[\n  \\left(\\prod_{g=1}^s Z_{g,0}\\right)F_v(z),\n  \\qquad\n  (-1)^{z_g}=Z_{g,0}Z_{g,1}.\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A random slice is sampled by choosing \\(J_0\\) uniformly from \\(\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\}\\), then, above depth \\(J_0\\), choosing one child pair uniformly and independently at every reached node and continuing through both children. Let \\(D\\) be the reached nodes at depth \\(J_0\\). Conditional on \\(J_0=j\\),\n  \\[\n  |D|=2^j,\\qquad\n  \\Pr[v\\in D]=s^{-j}\n  \\]\n  for every node \\(v\\) at depth \\(j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(F_{\\mathrm{out}}=(F_u)_{u\\notin D}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(F\\), the root output as a multilinear polynomial in the \\(k\\) leaf signs has only degree-\\(d\\) monomials. Each monomial traversal chooses one child from every pair at every node it reaches and meets every possible slice \\(D\\) at exactly one node. Consequently, negating all tables on a slice negates \\(W\\).</p><p>Let \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\) and \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\). Draw \\(F\\) uniformly and construct \\(F'\\) by keeping tables off \\(D\\) unchanged and coupling corresponding entries on \\(D\\) as independent uniform sign pairs of correlation \\(t\\). Independently draw \\(R\\in H^k\\) uniformly; obtain \\(R^1,R^2\\) by retaining each row with probability \\(r\\), otherwise replacing it by an independent uniform row, independently in the two copies. Then for every \\(a\\in A\\) and fixed slice,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[W(R^1,F)(a)W(R^2,F')(a)\\mid D]\n=\ntr^{2d}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nIf \\(r=1-\\mu/d\\) with \\(0&lt;\\mu&lt;1/2\\), then \\(r^{2d}\\ge1-2\\mu\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a fixed gate table, expand in its argument:\n\\[\nF_v(z)=\n\\sum_{I\\subseteq[s]}\\widehat F_v(I)(-1)^{\\sum_{g\\in I}z_g}.\n\\]\nSubstitution in (3.1) makes the \\(I\\)-term\n\\[\n\\widehat F_v(I)\n\\prod_{g\\notin I}Z_{g,0}\n\\prod_{g\\in I}Z_{g,1}.\n\\]\nIterating gives\n\\[\nW_F(x)=\n\\sum_{P\\in\\mathcal P}a_P(F)\\prod_{i\\in P}x_i,\n\\qquad |P|=d,\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal P\\) is the family of leaf sets produced by choosing one child per pair at every reached internal node.</p><p>The leaf set determines all choices: a selected child has a nonempty intersection with that set, whereas an unselected child has none. Different traversals therefore give different monomials. The coefficient \\(a_P(F)\\) is the product of one Walsh coefficient of \\(F_v\\) at every node reached by the traversal.</p><p>At the root both traversals are present. At each common node above the slice depth, exactly one child is selected by both. Induction gives exactly one common node at slice depth. Thus each \\(a_P\\) contains exactly one factor from a table on \\(D\\). Negating all such tables negates every coefficient and hence the word.</p><p>For each Walsh coefficient of a slice table,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[\\widehat F'_v(I)\\mid F_v]\n=\nt\\widehat F_v(I).\n\\]\nConditional independence between tables gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[a_P(F')\\mid F,D]=ta_P(F).\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(a\\), the signs \\(x_i^b=(-1)^{R_i^b(a)}\\) are uniform, independent across \\(i\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[x_i^1x_i^2]=r^2.\n\\]\nThe product expectation of two monomials in (3.3) is zero unless their leaf sets agree, and is \\(r^{2d}\\) when they agree. Since \\(W_F\\) is Boolean, Parseval gives\n\\[\n\\sum_Pa_P(F)^2=1.\n\\]\nAveraging first over rows and then over \\(F'\\) proves (3.2). Finally, Bernoulli’s inequality gives\n\\[\n(1-\\mu/d)^{2d}\\ge1-2\\mu.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\varphi\\) is odd, then \\(f(R,F)=\\varphi(W(R,F))\\) is odd under simultaneous negation of the slice tables. Independent row resampling preserves this identity. Thus both \\(f\\) and its row smoothing have mean zero in the slice entries when rows and outside tables are fixed. The cube in these entries has coordinates\n\\[\n(v,z)\\in D\\times\\mathbb F_2^s,\n\\]\ndistinct from the Walsh expansion of a fixed table in its argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, completeness; also the construction-size verification in Section 9", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\\ge1-a\\), the graph has a cut of value at least \\( (1-2Ta)\\frac{1+tr^{2d}}2 \\ge \\frac{1+t}2-t\\mu-2Ta. \\) For fixed \\(t,\\mu,s,m,n,T\\), this rationally weighted graph is constructible …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix rational \\(t\\in(0,1)\\), integers \\(s,m,T\\ge1\\) with \\(s\\ge2\\), rational \\(0&lt;\\mu&lt;1/2\\), and\n  \\[\n  k=(2s)^m,\\qquad d=s^m,\\qquad r=1-\\mu/d.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be an affine game as in Proposition 2, with edge law \\(\\lambda\\), label spaces \\(\\mathbb F_2^{n+1},\\mathbb F_2^n\\), and projections \\(\\pi_e\\). Remove endpoints with zero marginal probability when conditioning.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal B=(\\mathbb F_2^{n+1})^T,\\qquad\n  A=(\\mathbb F_2^n)^T,\\qquad\n  H=\\operatorname{Aff}(A,\\mathbb F_2).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(\\mathbf x\\in X^T\\), create a graph vertex \\((\\mathbf x,w)\\) for each \\(w\\in\\{-1,1\\}^{\\mathcal B}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Sample an edge by:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Drawing \\(\\mathbf y\\in Y^T\\) from the product right marginal, then independent edge tuples \\(\\mathbf e,\\mathbf e'\\) from the product conditional law given \\(\\mathbf y\\). Let \\(\\mathbf x,\\mathbf x'\\) be their left endpoints, and \\(\\pi_{\\mathbf e},\\pi_{\\mathbf e'}:\\mathcal B\\to A\\) their coordinatewise projections.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Independently drawing a slice \\(D\\) and tables \\(F,F'\\) as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Drawing uniform \\(R\\in H^k\\), then independent row-resampled copies \\(R^1,R^2\\) with retention \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. Outputting the unordered edge with endpoints\n     \\[\n     (\\mathbf x,W(R^1,F)\\circ\\pi_{\\mathbf e}),\n     \\qquad\n     (\\mathbf x',-W(R^2,F')\\circ\\pi_{\\mathbf e'}).\n     \\tag{4.1}\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Aggregate probabilities of outcomes having the same unordered endpoints.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\\ge1-a\\), the graph has a cut of value at least\n\\[\n(1-2Ta)\\frac{1+tr^{2d}}2\n\\ge\n\\frac{1+t}2-t\\mu-2Ta.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(t,\\mu,s,m,n,T\\), this rationally weighted graph is constructible deterministically in polynomial time and polynomial output size.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose game labels \\(\\ell_X,\\ell_Y\\) satisfying an edge with probability at least \\(1-a\\). Give each word vertex the evaluation sign\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\mathbf x}(w)\n=\nw(\\ell_X(x_1),\\ldots,\\ell_X(x_T)).\n\\]\nEach coordinate of either sampled edge tuple has the original edge law. By a union bound, all \\(2T\\) sampled constraints are satisfied except with probability at most \\(2Ta\\).</p><p>When they are all satisfied, both queried label tuples project to\n\\[\n\\mathbf l=(\\ell_Y(y_1),\\ldots,\\ell_Y(y_T))\\in A.\n\\]\nThe two cut signs have product\n\\[\n-W(R^1,F)(\\mathbf l)W(R^2,F')(\\mathbf l).\n\\]\nWord-generation data are independent of the game edges, so Proposition 3 gives conditional cut probability\n\\[\n(1+tr^{2d})/2.\n\\]\nThe remaining event contributes nonnegative cut probability. This proves the first bound when \\(2Ta\\le1\\); when \\(2Ta&gt;1\\), that bound is negative and automatic.</p><p>Use \\(r^{2d}\\ge1-2\\mu\\). The loss due to the prefactor is at most \\(2Ta\\), giving the second bound.</p><p>For effectivity, the vertex set has size\n\\[\n|X|^T2^{|\\mathcal B|}\n=\n|X|^T2^{\\,2^{(n+1)T}}.\n\\]\nEvery factor except \\(|X|\\) is constant for fixed parameters. There are polynomially many endpoint and edge tuples, and all word-generation sample spaces have constant size. Enumerate all outcomes and add each probability to the corresponding edge weight.</p><p>The initial game weights, \\(t\\), and \\(r\\) are rational. These weights and the conditional edge probabilities have polynomial bit complexity. All alphabets and words can be enumerated in constant time relative to the input size. The construction is therefore deterministic and polynomial.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, stability identity", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every cut, \\( \\operatorname{cut}(\\sigma) = \\frac12 + \\frac12\\mathbb E\\operatorname{Stab}^{F_D}_t(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}) \\frac12\\mathbb E\\operatorname{Stab}^{F_D}_t(\\overline A_{\\mathbf y}), \\) where expectations …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the graph test of Proposition 4, including its spaces \\(A,\\mathcal B,H\\), slice \\(D\\), and row retention \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An arbitrary cut is a family\n  \\[\n  \\sigma_{\\mathbf x}:\\{-1,1\\}^{\\mathcal B}\\to\\{-1,1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\sigma^{\\mathrm{even}}(w)=\\frac{\\sigma(w)+\\sigma(-w)}2,\n  \\qquad\n  \\sigma^{\\mathrm o}(w)=\\frac{\\sigma(w)-\\sigma(-w)}2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  G_{\\mathbf e}(R,F)\n  =\n  \\sigma^{\\mathrm o}_{\\mathbf x(\\mathbf e)}\n  (W(R,F)\\circ\\pi_{\\mathbf e}),\n  \\tag{4.3}\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  G_{\\mathbf y}(R,F)\n  =\n  \\mathbb E_{\\mathbf e\\mid\\mathbf y}G_{\\mathbf e}(R,F),\n  \\qquad\n  \\overline G_{\\mathbf y}=T_rG_{\\mathbf y},\n  \\tag{4.4}\n  \\]\n  where \\(T_r\\) independently retains each row with probability \\(r\\) and otherwise resamples it uniformly.</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(A_{\\mathbf y}\\) and \\(\\overline A_{\\mathbf y}=T_rA_{\\mathbf y}\\) analogously using \\(\\sigma^{\\mathrm{even}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The notation \\(\\operatorname{Stab}^{F_D}_t\\) means Boolean noise stability in the slice-table entries, with all other displayed variables fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every cut,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cut}(\\sigma)\n=\n\\frac12\n+\n\\frac12\\mathbb E\\operatorname{Stab}^{F_D}_t(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y})\n-\n\\frac12\\mathbb E\\operatorname{Stab}^{F_D}_t(\\overline A_{\\mathbf y}),\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nwhere expectations are over \\(\\mathbf y,D,R,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cut}(\\sigma)\n\\le\n\\frac12+\n\\frac12\\mathbb E\\operatorname{Stab}^{F_D}_t(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}).\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]</p><p>The functions \\(G_{\\mathbf y}\\) are bounded odd functions of the generated word:\n\\[\nG_{\\mathbf y}(R,F)\n=\n\\varphi_{\\mathbf y}(W(R,F)),\n\\qquad\n\\varphi_{\\mathbf y}(w)\n=\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf e\\mid\\mathbf y}\n\\sigma_{\\mathbf x(\\mathbf e)}^{\\mathrm o}(w\\circ\\pi_{\\mathbf e}).\n\\]\nConsequently \\(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}\\) has mean zero in the slice entries for each fixed \\(\\mathbf y,D,R,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Condition on \\(\\mathbf y,D,R,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\). The sampled edge tuples and two row resamplings are independent between copies. After averaging over them, the expected first sign, as a function of \\(F_D\\), is\n\\[\n\\overline A_{\\mathbf y}+\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}.\n\\]\nThe minus sign in the second queried word makes the expected second sign\n\\[\n\\overline A_{\\mathbf y}-\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}\n\\]\nat \\(F_D'\\).</p><p>The slice inputs are ordinary \\(t\\)-correlated uniform cube inputs. The rows in these functions are the common latent array; the two independent resamplings have been absorbed into \\(T_r\\).</p><p>Negating every slice entry leaves \\(\\overline A_{\\mathbf y}\\) unchanged and negates \\(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}\\), by Proposition 3. Their Fourier supports therefore have opposite total parities, so cross terms vanish. The expected sign product is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Stab}_t(\\overline A_{\\mathbf y})\n-\n\\operatorname{Stab}_t(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}).\n\\]\nAn edge is cut with probability one half of one minus its sign product, giving (4.5). Stability is nonnegative for \\(t&gt;0\\), which gives (4.6).</p><p>The displayed \\(\\varphi_{\\mathbf y}\\) is odd, bounded in absolute value by one, and depends on no slice or row data. Proposition 3 gives the asserted conditional mean-zero property. Thus a cut of value at least\n\\[\n\\frac{1+B(t)}2+\\frac\\delta2\n\\]\nhas expected odd slice stability at least \\(B(t)+\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, row symmetries", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every nonzero row Fourier coefficient satisfies \\( q_{v,g0}+q_{v,g1}=q_v \\qquad(g\\in[s]) \\) at every internal node \\(v\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A,H,s,m,k,d,W\\) be the affine space, row space, and tree code of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">A function of the code is\n  \\[\n  f(R,F)=\\varphi(W(R,F))\n  \\tag{3.4}\n  \\]\n  for a fixed real-valued function \\(\\varphi\\) on words.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(q\\in H^*\\), write \\(\\chi_q(h)=(-1)^{q(h)}\\). For \\(C=(q_1,\\ldots,q_k)\\in(H^*)^k\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\chi_C(R)=\\prod_i\\chi_{q_i}(R_i),\n  \\qquad\n  |C|=|\\{i:q_i\\ne0\\}|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the generator \\(P(v)\\) recursively: \\(P(v)=\\{v\\}\\) at a leaf, and\n  \\[\n  P(v)=P(v_{1,0})\\cup P(v_{1,1})\n  \\]\n  at an internal node. Put\n  \\[\n  q_v=\\sum_{i\\in P(v)}q_i,\n  \\]\n  and let \\(q_{v,gb}\\) denote the corresponding index of child \\((g,b)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At fixed tables,\n  \\[\n  f(R,F)=\\sum_C\\widehat f_C(F)\\chi_C(R).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F^{v,-}\\) negate \\(F_v\\) alone, and let \\(F^{v,z}\\) replace \\(F_v(u)\\) by \\(F_v(u+z)\\), for \\(z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nonzero row Fourier coefficient satisfies\n\\[\nq_{v,g0}+q_{v,g1}=q_v\n\\qquad(g\\in[s])\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nat every internal node \\(v\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\widehat f_C(F^{v,-})\n=\n(-1)^{q_v(1)}\\widehat f_C(F),\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\widehat f_C(F^{v,z})\n=\n(-1)^{\\sum_gz_gq_{v,g1}(1)}\\widehat f_C(F).\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nThese statements remain true after row smoothing, row Fourier projections, and projection to any range of total Fourier degrees in the entries of any specified collection of whole gate tables.</p><p>For row smoothing,\n\\[\n\\widehat{T_rf}_C(F)=r^{|C|}\\widehat f_C(F).\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nIt preserves \\([-1,1]\\), is convolution by independent increments that are zero with probability \\(r\\) and uniform rows with probability \\(1-r\\), and commutes with fixed row translations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Adding the same \\(h\\in H\\) to every row in \\(P(v)\\) multiplies the subtree output at \\(a\\) by \\((-1)^{h(a)}\\). This follows inductively from (3.1): multiplying both children of one pair by the same sign leaves all gate input bits unchanged and multiplies the prefactor by that sign.</p><p>For each \\(g\\), shifting the generators of both children of pair \\(g\\) by \\(h\\) has the same effect on the subtree at \\(v\\) as shifting \\(P(v)\\) by \\(h\\). Hence the two translations have the same effect on the whole word. Taking row Fourier coefficients yields\n\\[\n\\chi_{q_{v,g0}+q_{v,g1}}(h)\\widehat f_C(F)\n=\n\\chi_{q_v}(h)\\widehat f_C(F)\n\\qquad(h\\in H).\n\\]\nDistinct characters are distinct functions, proving (5.2).</p><p>Negating \\(F_v\\) has the same effect on the word as adding \\(1\\) to all rows in \\(P(v)\\), proving (5.3). To translate the input of \\(F_v\\) by \\(z\\), add \\(1\\) to the generator of its second child in each pair with \\(z_g=1\\). This changes precisely those input bits and leaves its prefactor unchanged. Fourier transformation gives (5.4).</p><p>Row smoothing and row Fourier projections act diagonally on \\(C\\), so preserve all identities. On the table-entry cube, a domain translation permutes coordinates, and gate negation multiplies each monomial by its parity sign. Both preserve total degree in any specified collection of whole gate tables and commute with the stated projections.</p><p>The multiplier formula for \\(T_r\\) follows from independent row resampling. Its convolution description, range preservation, and commutation with translations follow directly from that resampling law.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, average cost of the unsplit projection", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbb E_{J_0,D} \\sum_{v\\in D} \\|(I-S_v)\\overline f\\|_{2,R,F}^2 \\le \\frac{s}{2em\\mu}. \\) The same bound holds after averaging over additional background data determining \\(f\\), provided the slice is independent of …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the affine tree code and row indices of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">An index \\(C\\) is unsplit at \\(v\\) when, for every \\(g\\),\n  \\[\n  (q_{v,g0},q_{v,g1})\n  \\in\\{(q_v,0),(0,q_v)\\}.\n  \\]\n  If \\(q_v=0\\), this requires every child index to be zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_v\\) be the orthogonal row Fourier projection onto indices unsplit at \\(v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f=\\varphi(W)\\) with \\(|f|\\le1\\), let\n  \\[\n  r=1-\\mu/d,\\qquad0&lt;\\mu&lt;1/2,\\qquad\\overline f=T_rf,\n  \\]\n  and choose the random slice independently of \\(f\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E_{J_0,D}\n\\sum_{v\\in D}\n\\|(I-S_v)\\overline f\\|_{2,R,F}^2\n\\le\n\\frac{s}{2em\\mu}.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nThe same bound holds after averaging over additional background data determining \\(f\\), provided the slice is independent of those data.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix an index \\(C\\) satisfying (5.2). Let \\(N_j\\) count nodes at depth \\(j\\) with \\(q_v\\ne0\\), and let \\(B_j\\) count failures of the unsplit condition there.</p><p>If \\(q_v\\ne0\\), each child pair contains a nonzero index, and a failure supplies at least one additional nonzero child. If \\(q_v=0\\), a failure supplies at least two nonzero children, whereas its minimum contribution is zero. Thus\n\\[\nB_j\\le N_{j+1}-sN_j.\n\\]\nUsing slice inclusion probabilities and telescoping,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_{J_0,D}\n\\sum_{v\\in D}\\mathbf1_{\\{C\\text{ split at }v\\}}\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac1m\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\\frac{N_{j+1}-sN_j}{s^j}\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\frac{s}{m}\\left(\\frac{N_m}{s^m}-N_0\\right)\n\\le\n\\frac{s|C|}{md}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(N_m=|C|\\).</p><p>By the multiplier formula and Parseval, the left side of (5.5) is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{s}{m}\n\\sum_C\n\\left(r^{2|C|}\\frac{|C|}{d}\\right)\n\\mathbb E_F|\\widehat f_C(F)|^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nxe^{-2\\mu x}\\le(2e\\mu)^{-1},\n\\qquad\n\\log r\\le-\\mu/d.\n\\]\nEvery parenthesized factor is therefore at most \\((2e\\mu)^{-1}\\), while\n\\[\n\\sum_C\\mathbb E_F|\\widehat f_C(F)|^2\n=\n\\|f\\|_2^2\\le1.\n\\]\nThis proves the estimate. The argument is pointwise in additional background data, so averaging preserves it.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, free-shift coordinates", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The map \\( H^k\\longrightarrow H^{2s}\\times H^{k-2s}, \\qquad R\\longmapsto((L_{g,b}),\\Gamma) \\) is a linear bijection.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the tree generators, row indices, and unsplit projections of Propositions 6–7.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a slice \\(D\\) and a node \\(v\\in D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The \\(2s\\) sets \\(P(v_{g,b})\\) are nonempty and disjoint. Choose a representative leaf \\(i_{g,b}\\) from each, using only the tree, and put\n  \\[\n  L_{g,b}=R_{i_{g,b}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For every other leaf \\(i\\) in that generator, record \\(R_i+L_{g,b}\\); retain unchanged rows outside the generators. Denote these \\(k-2s\\) coordinates by \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map\n\\[\nH^k\\longrightarrow H^{2s}\\times H^{k-2s},\n\\qquad\nR\\longmapsto((L_{g,b}),\\Gamma)\n\\]\nis a linear bijection. Under uniform rows, the free shifts \\(L_{g,b}\\) are independent uniforms conditional on \\(\\Gamma\\). The shift Fourier index corresponding to a full row index \\(C\\) is\n\\[\nQ_{g,b}=q_{v,gb}.\n\\]</p><p>At fixed \\(\\Gamma\\), \\(S_v\\) is exactly the shift Fourier projection onto unsplit shift indices. For a function of the code or its row smoothing, coefficients vanish unless\n\\[\nQ_{g,0}+Q_{g,1}\n\\]\nis the same for every \\(g\\). The table identities (5.3)–(5.4) hold for every shift coefficient at fixed context, with\n\\[\nq_v=Q_{1,0}+Q_{1,1},\\qquad q_{v,g1}=Q_{g,1}.\n\\]</p><p>On the part odd in \\(F_v\\), the common index \\(q\\) is a label. Its unsplit shift indices are uniquely indexed by \\(I\\subseteq[s]\\), with character\n\\[\n\\prod_{g\\notin I}\\chi_q(L_{g,0})\n\\prod_{g\\in I}\\chi_q(L_{g,1}).\n\\]\nTranslation of the domain of \\(F_v\\) by \\(z\\) multiplies the corresponding coefficient by\n\\[\n(-1)^{\\sum_{g\\in I}z_g}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The inverse map sets each representative row to \\(L_{g,b}\\) and recovers other rows in its generator by adding \\(L_{g,b}\\) to their recorded differences. This proves bijectivity.</p><p>In a row character, the coefficient of \\(L_{g,b}\\) is\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in P(v_{g,b})}q_i=q_{v,gb}.\n\\]\nThus the unsplit condition depends only on the shift index \\(Q\\), not on any Fourier index of \\(\\Gamma\\). Group the full Fourier expansion by \\(Q\\). This gives the fiberwise action of \\(S_v\\), the common-sum support, and the table-action identities: all full coefficients with a given \\(Q\\) carry the same signs.</p><p>For nonzero common \\(q\\), an unsplit index chooses the second child in a subset \\(I\\) and the first elsewhere. If \\(q=0\\), there is only the all-zero unsplit index. Gate negation multiplies its coefficient by \\((-1)^{q(1)}\\). On the odd part, \\(q(1)=1\\), so \\(q\\) is a label and is nonzero. The description by \\(q,I\\) is therefore unique. Formula (5.4) gives the final translation character.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, vector-valued hashing", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbb E\\sum_{z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s}\\|U(z)\\|^2=M. \\) For every \\(\\zeta>0\\), there are \\(\\gamma>0\\) and \\(s_0\\in\\mathbb N\\), depending only on \\(\\zeta\\), such that, for every \\(s\\ge s_0\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a finite-dimensional vector space over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), and let\n  \\[\n  \\chi_q(u)=(-1)^{q(u)}\n  \\qquad(q\\in H^*,\\ u\\in H).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal V\\) be a real Hilbert space and \\(s\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c(q,z)\\in\\mathcal V\\), for \\(q\\in H^*\\), \\(z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s\\), and define unnormalized sums\n  \\[\n  w_q=\\sum_{z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s}\\|c(q,z)\\|^2,\n  \\qquad\n  M=\\sum_{q\\in H^*}w_q.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(L,J_1,\\ldots,J_s\\) independently and uniformly in \\(H\\), and define\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf J(q)=(q(J_1),\\ldots,q(J_s)),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  U(z)=\n  \\sum_{q\\in H^*}\\chi_q(L)c(q,z+\\mathbf J(q)).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s}\\|U(z)\\|^2=M.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), there are \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) and \\(s_0\\in\\mathbb N\\), depending only on \\(\\zeta\\), such that, for every \\(s\\ge s_0\\),\n\\[\n\\max_qw_q\\le\\gamma M\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\mathbb E\\max_z\\|U(z)\\|^2\\le\\zeta M.\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nThe constants are independent of the dimensions of \\(H\\) and \\(\\mathcal V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>All expectations over \\(H\\) and \\(\\mathbb F_2^s\\) use uniform probability measure; displayed sums defining \\(w_q,M\\) are unnormalized. Conditional on the \\(J_g\\), orthogonality in \\(L\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_L\\|U(z)\\|^2\n=\n\\sum_q\\|c(q,z+\\mathbf J(q))\\|^2.\n\\]\nSumming over \\(z\\) proves (6.1). If \\(M=0\\), all vectors vanish, so assume \\(M&gt;0\\).</p><p>Put \\(h=2^s\\) and \\(\\rho=\\max_qw_q/M\\). For arbitrary \\(C,D&gt;0\\), the proof establishes\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\max_z\\|U(z)\\|^2\n\\le\n2M\\left[\n\\sqrt{2\\rho+\\frac{2C^2}{D^2}+\\frac{3D^4}{h}}\n+\n\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt C}+\\frac1D\\right)^2\n+\n\\frac1h\n\\right].\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(p\\in\\mathbb F_2^s\\), \\(u\\in H\\), define\n\\[\n\\alpha_q(p)=\\sum_z(-1)^{p\\cdot z}c(q,z),\n\\qquad\nA_p(u)=\\sum_q\\chi_q(u)\\alpha_q(p),\n\\]\n\\[\nS_p=L+\\sum_{g=1}^sp_gJ_g.\n\\]\nFourier inversion in \\(z\\) gives\n\\[\nU(z)=\\mathbb E_p(-1)^{p\\cdot z}A_p(S_p).\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nWriting \\(m_p=\\mathbb E_u\\|A_p(u)\\|^2\\), Parseval in both groups gives\n\\[\nm_p=\\sum_q\\|\\alpha_q(p)\\|^2,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_pm_p=M,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_p\\|\\alpha_q(p)\\|^2=w_q.\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]</p><p>For distinct \\(p,p'\\), \\(S_p,S_{p'}\\) are independent uniforms, because \\((1,p),(1,p')\\) are linearly independent over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\). Any three distinct \\(p_1,p_2,p_3\\) likewise give three independent uniforms. Any nonempty subcollection of \\((1,p_i)\\) summing to zero must have even size, and among three distinct vectors would therefore consist of two equal vectors.</p><p>Define the two truncations together by\n\\[\nA'_p(u)=\n\\begin{cases}\nA_p(u),&amp;m_p\\le CM\\text{ and }\\|A_p(u)\\|\\le D\\sqrt M,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\alpha'_q(p)=\\mathbb E_u\\chi_q(u)A'_p(u),\n\\qquad\nw'_q=\\mathbb E_p\\|\\alpha'_q(p)\\|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nU'(z)=\\mathbb E_p(-1)^{p\\cdot z}A'_p(S_p).\n\\]\nPointwise truncation does not increase total squared norm, so\n\\[\n\\sum_qw'_q\\le M.\n\\]\nIf \\(m_p\\le CM\\), then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\alpha_q(p)-\\alpha'_q(p)\\|\n&amp;\\le\n\\mathbb E_u\\|A_p(u)\\|\n\\mathbf1_{\\{\\|A_p(u)\\|&gt;D\\sqrt M\\}}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac{m_p}{D\\sqrt M}\n\\le\n\\frac{C\\sqrt M}{D}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIf \\(m_p&gt;CM\\), then \\(\\alpha'_q(p)=0\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\sum_qw'_q\\le M,\\qquad\nw'_q\\le2w_q+\\frac{2C^2M}{D^2}.\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nX_p=\\|(A_p-A'_p)(S_p)\\|,\n\\qquad X=\\mathbb E_pX_p.\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality in (6.4) implies\n\\[\n\\max_z\\|U(z)-U'(z)\\|\\le X.\n\\]\nThe contribution to \\(\\mathbb EX\\) from \\(m_p&gt;CM\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_p\\mathbf1_{\\{m_p&gt;CM\\}}\\sqrt{m_p}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\mathbb E_pm_p}{\\sqrt{CM}}\n=\n\\sqrt{M/C}.\n\\]\nThe contribution from the remaining indices is at most\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_pm_p/(D\\sqrt M)=\\sqrt M/D.\n\\]\nThe \\(X_p\\) are pairwise independent. Since \\(A_p-A'_p\\) is pointwise either \\(A_p\\) or zero,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(X)\n=\nh^{-2}\\sum_p\\operatorname{Var}(X_p)\n\\le\nh^{-2}\\sum_p\\mathbb E_u\\|A_p(u)-A'_p(u)\\|^2\n\\le M/h.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\max_z\\|U(z)-U'(z)\\|^2\n\\le\nM\\left[\n\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt C}+\\frac1D\\right)^2+\\frac1h\n\\right].\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]</p><p>For the fourth moment, put \\(B_p=A'_p(S_p)\\). Expansion and summation of characters in \\(z\\) give the exact identity\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\sum_z\\|U'(z)\\|^4\n&amp;=\nh^{-3}\\sum_{p_1,p_2,p_3}\n\\mathbb E[\n\\langle B_{p_1},B_{p_2}\\rangle\n\\langle B_{p_3},B_{p_1+p_2+p_3}\\rangle]\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E_{p_1,p_2,p_3}\n\\mathbb E[\n\\langle B_{p_1},B_{p_2}\\rangle\n\\langle B_{p_3},B_{p_4}\\rangle],\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(6.8)\n\\]\nwhere \\(p_4=p_1+p_2+p_3\\), and \\(p_1,p_2,p_3\\) are independent uniform indices. The factor \\(h^{-3}\\) comes from four Fourier-inversion factors \\(h^{-1}\\) and a sum of \\(h\\) characters.</p><p>If any two of \\(p_1,p_2,p_3,p_4\\) coincide, some two of the first three coincide. This event has probability at most \\(3/h\\). Since \\(\\|B_p\\|\\le D\\sqrt M\\), its contribution to (6.8) is at most \\(3D^4M^2/h\\) in absolute value.</p><p>For four distinct indices, \\(S_{p_1},S_{p_2},S_{p_3}\\) are independent uniform, and\n\\[\nS_{p_4}=S_{p_1}+S_{p_2}+S_{p_3}.\n\\]\nExpanding the four \\(A'_{p_i}\\) in characters gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[\n\\langle B_{p_1},B_{p_2}\\rangle\n\\langle B_{p_3},B_{p_4}\\rangle]\n=\n\\sum_q\n\\langle\\alpha'_q(p_1),\\alpha'_q(p_2)\\rangle\n\\langle\\alpha'_q(p_3),\\alpha'_q(p_4)\\rangle.\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nIndeed, indices \\(q_1,q_2,q_3,q_4\\) produce\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=1}^3\\chi_{q_i+q_4}(S_{p_i}),\n\\]\nwhose expectation vanishes unless all four indices agree.</p><p>The right side of (6.9) need not be nonnegative. For an upper bound, replace each summand by\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=1}^4\\|\\alpha'_q(p_i)\\|,\n\\]\nand drop the distinctness restriction. For each fixed \\(q\\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_{p_1,p_2,p_3}\n\\prod_{i=1}^4\\|\\alpha'_q(p_i)\\|\n&amp;\\le\n\\left(\n\\mathbb E\\|\\alpha'_q(p_1)\\|^2\\|\\alpha'_q(p_2)\\|^2\n\\right)^{1/2}\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\cdot\n\\left(\n\\mathbb E\\|\\alpha'_q(p_3)\\|^2\\|\\alpha'_q(p_4)\\|^2\n\\right)^{1/2}\\\\\n&amp;=(w'_q)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last equality uses the unconditioned independent uniform law of each pair, not independence after conditioning on distinctness.</p><p>Together with (6.6),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\sum_z\\|U'(z)\\|^4\n&amp;\\le\n\\sum_q(w'_q)^2+\\frac{3D^4M^2}{h}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\nM^2\\left(\n2\\rho+\\frac{2C^2}{D^2}+\\frac{3D^4}{h}\n\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\max_z\\|U'(z)\\|^2\n\\le\n\\left(\\mathbb E\\sum_z\\|U'(z)\\|^4\\right)^{1/2}\n\\le\nM\\sqrt{2\\rho+\\frac{2C^2}{D^2}+\\frac{3D^4}{h}}.\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]\nUse \\((a+b)^2\\le2a^2+2b^2\\), (6.7), and (6.10) to obtain (6.3).</p><p>Finally set \\(\\epsilon=\\min\\{\\zeta,1\\}\\), and choose\n\\[\nC=\\frac{64}{\\epsilon},\\qquad\nD=\\frac{8C}{\\epsilon},\\qquad\n\\gamma=\\frac{\\epsilon^2}{128},\n\\]\n\\[\n2^{s_0}\\ge\n\\max\\left\\{\\frac{192D^4}{\\epsilon^2},\\frac{16}{\\epsilon}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(s\\ge s_0\\) and \\(\\rho\\le\\gamma\\), the square-root term in (6.3) is at most \\(\\epsilon/4\\). Since \\(D\\ge\\sqrt C\\), its other two bracketed terms are each at most \\(\\epsilon/16\\). The resulting bound is\n\\[\n3\\epsilon M/4\\le\\zeta M,\n\\]\nas required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, dimension-independent extraction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are \\(s\\ge2\\), \\(m\\ge1\\), and \\(p_*,\\theta>0\\), depending only on \\(t,\\delta,\\mu\\), such that \\( \\mathbb E_{\\omega,D,R,F_{\\mathrm{out}}} \\operatorname{Stab}^{F_D}_t(\\overline f_\\omega) \\ge B(t)+\\delta \\) implies …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  t\\in(0,1),\\qquad\n  0&lt;\\delta&lt;1-B(t),\\qquad\n  0&lt;\\mu&lt;1/2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be any finite-dimensional affine binary space, \\(H=\\operatorname{Aff}(A,\\mathbb F_2)\\), and use the tree code of Proposition 3 with parameters \\(s,m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\omega\\) be an additional random parameter independent of all rows, tables, and the random slice.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\varphi_\\omega:\\{-1,1\\}^A\\to[-1,1],\n  \\qquad\n  \\varphi_\\omega(-w)=-\\varphi_\\omega(w).\n  \\]\n  Set\n  \\[\n  f_\\omega(R,F)=\\varphi_\\omega(W(R,F)),\n  \\quad\n  \\overline f_\\omega=T_rf_\\omega,\n  \\quad\n  r=1-\\mu/d.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v\\in D\\), use the coordinates \\(R\\leftrightarrow(L,\\Gamma)\\) of Proposition 8. A hat marked “shifts” expands only \\(L\\in H^{2s}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a label \\(q\\in A=\\{q\\in H^*:q(1)=1\\}\\), define\n  \\[\n  m_q(f;\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}})\n  =\n  \\mathbb E_{F_D}\n  \\sum_{\\substack{Q\\in(H^*)^{2s}\\\\\n  Q_{g0}+Q_{g1}=q\\ \\forall g}}\n  |\\widehat f^{\\,\\mathrm{shifts}}(Q)|^2.\n  \\tag{7.1}\n  \\]\n  The dependence on \\(\\omega,D,v\\) is suppressed. If \\(|f|\\le1\\), then at every fixed context\n  \\[\n  0\\le\\sum_{q\\in A}m_q(f;\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}})\\le1.\n  \\tag{7.2}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(s\\ge2\\), \\(m\\ge1\\), and \\(p_*,\\theta&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(t,\\delta,\\mu\\), such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\omega,D,R,F_{\\mathrm{out}}}\n\\operatorname{Stab}^{F_D}_t(\\overline f_\\omega)\n\\ge B(t)+\\delta\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nimplies\n\\[\n\\Pr\\left[\n\\max_{q\\in A}\nm_q(\\overline f_\\omega;\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}})\n\\ge\\theta\n\\right]\\ge p_*.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nIn this probability, sample \\(\\omega,D\\), then a uniform node \\(v\\in D\\), and then independent uniform \\(F_{\\mathrm{out}},\\Gamma\\). The constants do not depend on the dimension of \\(A\\) or the law of \\(\\omega\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(K,\\tau,\\eta,\\Lambda\\) as in Lemma 10.1, depending only on \\(t,\\delta\\). Choose \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\n\\zeta K\\le\\Lambda/4.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 9 to obtain \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) and a lower bound on \\(s\\). Choose \\(s\\ge2\\) large enough both for that proposition and for\n\\[\nK2^{-s}\\le\\Lambda/4.\n\\]\nThen choose \\(m\\) large enough that\n\\[\ns/(2em\\mu)\\le\\eta\\tau/8.\n\\]\nAll three lemmas now apply. Define\n\\[\nM_0=\\frac{\\Lambda}{4\\cdot2^m},\n\\qquad\n\\theta=\\frac{\\gamma M_0}{K},\n\\qquad\np_*=\\frac{\\Lambda}{4K2^m}.\n\\tag{7.14}\n\\]</p><p>For each node, condition on \\(\\omega,D,v,\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) and integrate free shifts. Partition contexts into:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\mathrm{(i)}&amp;w_q\\le\\gamma M_v\\text{ for every }q;\\\\\n\\mathrm{(ii)}&amp;\\mathrm{(i)}\\text{ fails and }M_v&lt;M_0;\\\\\n\\mathrm{(iii)}&amp;M_v\\ge M_0\\text{ and }w_q&gt;\\gamma M_v\n\\text{ for some }q.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>On class (i), Proposition 9 bounds expected maximum odd influence by \\(\\zeta M_v\\). Summing and using (7.13) costs at most \\(\\Lambda/4\\).</p><p>On class (ii), the maximum is bounded in expectation by total influence \\(M_v&lt;M_0\\). Since \\(|D|\\le2^m\\), these contexts cost at most \\(2^mM_0=\\Lambda/4\\).</p><p>On class (iii), the same total-mass bound is at most \\(K\\) per node. Lemma 10.2 bounds all even contributions by \\(\\Lambda/4\\).</p><p>Let \\(N_{\\mathrm{iii}}\\) count nodes whose induced contexts lie in class (iii). Comparing these contributions with (7.6),\n\\[\n\\Lambda\n\\le\nK2^{-s}+\\zeta K+2^mM_0+K\\mathbb EN_{\\mathrm{iii}}\n\\le\n3\\Lambda/4+K\\mathbb EN_{\\mathrm{iii}}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\mathbb EN_{\\mathrm{iii}}\\ge\\Lambda/(4K).\n\\]\nAt each such node, (7.12) supplies a label with\n\\[\nm_q&gt;\\gamma M_0/K=\\theta.\n\\]\nChoosing a uniform node of the slice loses at most a factor \\(2^m\\), yielding probability at least \\(p_*\\). This proves (7.4), with constants independent of \\(A\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, Step 1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in D} \\max_{z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s}\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v) \\ge\\Lambda, \\) and \\( \\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in D}\\sum_{z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s} \\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v) \\le K. \\) Expectations are over …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the family \\(f_\\omega,\\overline f_\\omega\\) and excess-stability assumption (7.3) of Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Temporarily let \\(s\\ge2,m\\ge1\\) be arbitrary.</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Proposition 1 with \\(\\xi=\\delta/2\\), obtaining \\(K\\ge1\\) and \\(0&lt;\\tau\\le1\\). Put\n  \\[\n  \\eta=\\delta/2,\\qquad \\Lambda=\\eta\\tau/8.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  H_v=P_{\\le K}^{F_D}S_v\\overline f_\\omega,\n  \\]\n  with \\(S_v\\) the unsplit row projection.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  \\frac{s}{2em\\mu}\\le\\eta\\tau/8.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in D}\n\\max_{z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s}\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v)\n\\ge\\Lambda,\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in D}\\sum_{z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s}\n\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v)\n\\le K.\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nExpectations are over \\(\\omega,D,R,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\), and influences are in the slice bits.</p>", "proof": "<p>Simultaneous negation of slice tables negates the word. Since \\(\\varphi_\\omega\\) is odd and smoothing preserves this identity, \\(\\overline f_\\omega\\) has mean zero in the slice bits for fixed \\(\\omega,D,R,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\). It lies in \\([-1,1]\\).</p><p>By Proposition 1 and the bound \\(\\operatorname{Stab}_t\\le1\\), (7.3) implies probability at least \\(\\eta=\\delta/2\\) that some entry of some \\(v\\in D\\) has\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inf}^{\\le K}_{v,z}(\\overline f_\\omega)\\ge\\tau.\n\\]\nIndeed, if this event has probability \\(p\\), expected stability is at most \\(B(t)+\\delta/2+p\\).</p><p>Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in D}\n\\|(I-S_v)\\overline f_\\omega\\|_{2,R,F}^2\n\\le\n\\frac{s}{2em\\mu}\n\\le\\eta\\tau/8.\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nEquivalently, this averages conditional squared norms in \\(F_D\\) over the remaining variables. Markov’s inequality shows that their sum is at most \\(\\tau/4\\), except on an event of probability \\(\\eta/2\\).</p><p>On the intersection with the influential-entry event, the triangle inequality for the projection defining influence gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v)}\n&amp;\\ge\n\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Inf}^{\\le K}_{v,z}(\\overline f_\\omega)}\n-\n\\|(I-S_v)\\overline f_\\omega\\|_{2,F_D}\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\sqrt\\tau/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe intersection has probability at least \\(\\eta/2\\), so (7.6) follows.</p><p>For (7.7), integrate over all rows. Each \\(S_v\\) is an orthogonal row projection and commutes with the slice-bit projections, so it can only decrease integrated influence at its entries. Drop each \\(S_v\\) separately. The resulting sum is bounded by total influence of\n\\[\nP_{\\le K}^{F_D}\\overline f_\\omega.\n\\]\nThe degree bound and \\(|\\overline f_\\omega|\\le1\\) give (7.7). No pointwise boundedness of \\(H_v\\) is asserted or needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, Step 2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each entry, \\( \\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v) = \\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v^{\\mathrm{even}}) + \\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v^{\\mathrm{odd}}). \\) Moreover …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(H_v,K\\) from Lemma 10.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Split \\(H_v=H_v^{\\mathrm{even}}+H_v^{\\mathrm{odd}}\\) according to parity under negation of \\(F_v\\) alone.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each entry,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v)\n=\n\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v^{\\mathrm{even}})\n+\n\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v^{\\mathrm{odd}}).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in D}\n\\max_z\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v^{\\mathrm{even}})\n\\le K2^{-s}.\n\\tag{7.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The even and odd parts have disjoint slice Fourier supports, also after retaining monomials containing a specified entry, proving influence additivity.</p><p>By Proposition 6, the even part has \\(q_v(1)=0\\) on row support. Since the index is unsplit, every child index is \\(0\\) or \\(q_v\\), and also takes value zero on \\(1\\). The domain-translation action (5.4) therefore makes the even part invariant under translations of the domain of \\(F_v\\), with rows and outside tables fixed. These translations act transitively on entries, so all entry influences at \\(v\\) are equal. There are \\(2^s\\) entries. Apply (7.7) to obtain (7.8).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, Step 3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are Hilbert-space vectors \\(c(q,z)\\), zero for functionals \\(q\\) that are not labels, such that \\( \\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v^{\\mathrm{odd}}) = \\left\\| \\sum_q\\chi_q(L_\\Sigma)c(q,z+\\mathbf J(q)) \\right\\|^2. \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(H_v^{\\mathrm{odd}}\\), \\(K\\), and masses \\(m_q\\) from Proposition 10 and Lemmas 10.1–10.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\omega,D,v,\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\); the \\(2s\\) free shifts remain independent uniform elements of \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  L_\\Sigma=\\sum_{g=1}^sL_{g0},\n  \\qquad\n  J_g=L_{g0}+L_{g1},\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathbf J(q)=(q(J_1),\\ldots,q(J_s)).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are Hilbert-space vectors \\(c(q,z)\\), zero for functionals \\(q\\) that are not labels, such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v^{\\mathrm{odd}})\n=\n\\left\\|\n\\sum_q\\chi_q(L_\\Sigma)c(q,z+\\mathbf J(q))\n\\right\\|^2.\n\\tag{7.11}\n\\]\nWriting\n\\[\nw_q=\\sum_z\\|c(q,z)\\|^2,\\qquad M_v=\\sum_qw_q,\n\\]\none has, at each context,\n\\[\nw_q\\le K\\,m_q(\\overline f_\\omega;\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}}),\n\\qquad M_v\\le K.\n\\tag{7.12}\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in D}M_v\n=\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in D}\\sum_z\n\\operatorname{Inf}_{v,z}(H_v^{\\mathrm{odd}})\n\\le K.\n\\tag{7.13}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On the odd part, the common pair sum \\(q\\) is a label and is nonzero. An unsplit shift index places \\(q\\) in the second child on a subset \\(I\\subseteq[s]\\), and in the first elsewhere. Let \\(a_{q,I}(F_D)\\) be its coefficient in \\(H_v^{\\mathrm{odd}}\\).</p><p>The \\(s+1\\) forms \\(L_\\Sigma,J_1,\\ldots,J_s\\) are independent uniform: the displayed linear map from \\(H^{2s}\\) onto \\(H^{s+1}\\) is surjective. The other \\(s-1\\) coordinates do not occur in the unsplit expansion. The shift character is\n\\[\n\\chi_q(L_\\Sigma)(-1)^{\\sum_{g\\in I}q(J_g)}.\n\\]\nProposition 8 gives\n\\[\na_{q,I}(F_v(\\cdot+z),F_{D\\setminus\\{v\\}})\n=\n(-1)^{\\sum_{g\\in I}z_g}a_{q,I}(F_D).\n\\]\nWith\n\\[\n\\Psi_q=\\sum_{I\\subseteq[s]}a_{q,I},\n\\]\nthis yields\n\\[\nH_v^{\\mathrm{odd}}\n=\n\\sum_{q\\in A}\n\\chi_q(L_\\Sigma)\n\\Psi_q(F_v(\\cdot+\\mathbf J(q)),F_{D\\setminus\\{v\\}}).\n\\tag{7.9}\n\\]</p><p>For distinct \\(I\\), the functions \\(a_{q,I}\\) are orthogonal in uniform slice bits: a domain translation distinguishing their characters preserves the inner product and changes its sign. Therefore\n\\[\n\\|\\Psi_q\\|_{2,F_D}^2\n=\n\\sum_I\\|a_{q,I}\\|_{2,F_D}^2\n\\le\nm_q(\\overline f_\\omega;\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}}).\n\\tag{7.10}\n\\]\nHere the unsplit projection is determined by the shift index, and degree and parity projections are orthogonal in the slice bits at fixed context. Each \\(\\Psi_q\\) has degree at most \\(K\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\widehat\\Psi_q(B,\\mathcal D)\\) be the slice Fourier coefficient whose entry set at \\(v\\) is \\(B\\subseteq\\mathbb F_2^s\\) and whose entry set on other slice gates is \\(\\mathcal D\\). For \\(z\\in\\mathbb F_2^s\\), define a vector, with coordinates indexed by \\((\\mathcal A,\\mathcal D)\\) satisfying \\(0\\in\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\mathbb F_2^s\\), by\n\\[\nc(q,z)_{\\mathcal A,\\mathcal D}\n=\n\\widehat\\Psi_q(z+\\mathcal A,\\mathcal D).\n\\]\nSet it to zero for nonlabels. Entry sets relative to \\(z\\) make the vector space the same for every \\(z,q\\).</p><p>A Fourier set containing \\(z\\) is uniquely \\(z+\\mathcal A\\) with \\(0\\in\\mathcal A\\). Translation by \\(\\mathbf J(q)\\) changes its coefficient to\n\\[\n\\widehat\\Psi_q(z+\\mathcal A+\\mathbf J(q),\\mathcal D).\n\\]\nTaking the squared norm of the coefficient vector in (7.9) proves (7.11).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_q\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{B,\\mathcal D}|B|\n|\\widehat\\Psi_q(B,\\mathcal D)|^2\\\\\n&amp;\\le K\\|\\Psi_q\\|_2^2\n\\le K\\,m_q(\\overline f_\\omega;\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe factor \\(|B|\\) counts entries whose influences contain the monomial, and is at most its total degree \\(K\\). Equation (7.2) gives \\(M_v\\le K\\). Proposition 9’s total-mass identity and (7.11) give (7.13), using (7.7). Free shifts are integrated separately for each node; their different coordinate systems preserve the uniform row law.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma 8.1, removing smoothing; includes the choice (8.2) and consequence (8.3)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(\\mathbf y,D,v,\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) and label \\(q\\in A\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the graph test and cut functions of Propositions 4–5:\n  \\[\n  A=(\\mathbb F_2^n)^T,\\quad\n  H=\\operatorname{Aff}(A,\\mathbb F_2),\\quad\n  G_{\\mathbf e},\\quad\n  \\overline G_{\\mathbf y}=T_r\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf e\\mid\\mathbf y}G_{\\mathbf e}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(D,v\\), fix the free-shift coordinate change \\(R\\leftrightarrow(L,\\Gamma)\\) using a deterministic tree-only choice of representatives.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R_i^{\\mathrm{new}}\\) be independent uniform rows in \\(H\\), and independently let\n  \\[\n  \\Pr(B_i=1)=1-r.\n  \\]\n  Set\n  \\[\n  E_i=B_iR_i^{\\mathrm{new}},\n  \\qquad E=(E_i)_{i=1}^k,\n  \\]\n  and let \\((E_L,E_\\Gamma)\\) be its free-shift coordinates.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the masses of (7.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(\\mathbf y,D,v,\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) and label \\(q\\in A\\),\n\\[\nm_q(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y};\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}})\n\\le\n\\mathbb E_{E,\\mathbf e\\mid\\mathbf y}\nm_q(G_{\\mathbf e};\\Gamma+E_\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}}).\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\nFix an ordering of labels and choose\n\\[\nq^*\n=\nq^*(\\mathbf y,D,v,\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}})\n\\in\n\\operatorname*{arg\\,max}_{q\\in A}\nm_q(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y};\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}})\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nby that ordering.</p><p>If the extraction conclusion (7.4) holds for \\(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}\\) with constants \\(p_*,\\theta\\), then, under the original unplanted law,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\nm_{q^*}(G_{\\mathbf e};\\Gamma+E_\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}})\n\\ge p_*\\theta.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\nThe score in this expectation lies in \\([0,1]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed context, regard functions of \\((L,F_D)\\) as a real Hilbert space with uniform probability measure. Let \\(\\Pi_q\\) project onto shift indices satisfying\n\\[\nQ_{g0}+Q_{g1}=q\n\\qquad(g\\in[s]).\n\\]\nThe mass \\(m_q\\) is the squared norm of this projection.</p><p>For any fixed row array, \\(R+E\\) has the one-copy row-resampling law. Therefore\n\\[\n\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}(L,\\Gamma,F)\n=\n\\mathbb E_{E,\\mathbf e\\mid\\mathbf y}\nG_{\\mathbf e}(L+E_L,\\Gamma+E_\\Gamma,F).\n\\]\nConvexity of squared Hilbert-space norm proves (8.1): translation by \\(E_L\\) commutes with \\(\\Pi_q\\) and preserves its norm, multiplying every shift Fourier coefficient by a sign.</p><p>The definition of \\(q^*\\) uses the original conditional edge average and original smoothing operator. It will remain unchanged under later modifications of row-data distributions.</p><p>Apply (8.1) to this maximizing label, and average the extraction conclusion. With probability at least \\(p_*\\), its mass is at least \\(\\theta\\), so (8.3) follows. Boundedness of \\(G_{\\mathbf e}\\) and Parseval put each score in \\([0,1]\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 8.2, hiding the planted coordinate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{TV}(\\mathsf P,\\mathsf U) \\le \\frac12\\sqrt{\\frac{1-p_0}{Tp_0}} \\le \\frac1{2\\sqrt{Tp_0}}. \\) If (8.3) holds and \\( T\\ge\\frac1{p_0(p_*\\theta)^2}, \\) then …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 11’s sampling law, with \\(A=(\\mathbb F_2^n)^T\\), \\(k\\) leaf rows, and \\(k-2s\\) context rows.</p><p class=\"li\">Each affine row has one constant coefficient and \\(T\\) blocks of \\(n\\) linear coefficients.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(a\\in[T]\\), let \\(Z_a\\) be the event that slot-\\(a\\) blocks vanish in all context rows and all \\(k\\) latent rows \\(R_i^{\\mathrm{new}}\\). Constants are unrestricted.</p><p class=\"li\">Under the unplanted law \\(\\mathsf U\\),\n  \\[\n  p_0=\\Pr(Z_a)=2^{-n(2k-2s)}.\n  \\tag{8.4}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The planted experiment chooses a uniform mark \\(a_*\\in[T]\\) and sets these blocks to zero there, leaving all other coefficients, masks, outside tables, and game edges with their original laws. Conditional on \\(a_*=a\\), this is \\(\\mathsf U\\) conditioned on \\(Z_a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathsf P\\) denote the planted law after forgetting the mark, on the full data\n  \\[\n  (\\mathbf e,J_0,D,v,F_{\\mathrm{out}},\\Gamma,R^{\\mathrm{new}},B).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The maximizing label \\(q^*\\) remains the fixed function (8.2), defined using the original smoothing and conditional edge average.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}(\\mathsf P,\\mathsf U)\n\\le\n\\frac12\\sqrt{\\frac{1-p_0}{Tp_0}}\n\\le\n\\frac1{2\\sqrt{Tp_0}}.\n\\tag{8.5}\n\\]\nIf (8.3) holds and\n\\[\nT\\ge\\frac1{p_0(p_*\\theta)^2},\n\\tag{8.6}\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathsf P}\nm_{q^*}(G_{\\mathbf e};\\Gamma+E_\\Gamma,F_{\\mathrm{out}})\n\\ge p_*\\theta/2.\n\\tag{8.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Conditional on depth, slice, and node, the events \\(Z_a\\) are independent: they concern disjoint blocks of independent uniform coefficients. Their common probability is \\(p_0\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nZ=\\sum_{a=1}^T\\mathbf1_{Z_a}.\n\\]\nAveraging conditional densities over the uniform mark gives\n\\[\n\\frac{d\\mathsf P}{d\\mathsf U}=\\frac{Z}{Tp_0}.\n\\]\nConditional on the tree data, \\(Z\\) is binomial with parameters \\(T,p_0\\), which do not depend on those data. Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}(\\mathsf P,\\mathsf U)\n=\n\\frac12\\mathbb E_{\\mathsf U}\n\\left|\\frac{Z}{Tp_0}-1\\right|\n\\le\n\\frac12\\sqrt{\\frac{1-p_0}{Tp_0}}\n\\]\nby Cauchy–Schwarz.</p><p>The score in (8.3) is a \\([0,1]\\)-valued function of the displayed data without the mark. In particular, \\(q^*\\) does not use the mark, the sampled incident edges beyond their \\(Y\\)-endpoints, or the sampled noise. Its statistical dependence on zero slots causes no difficulty.</p><p>Expectations of this score under the two laws differ by at most their total variation distance. Equations (8.3) and (8.6) therefore give (8.7).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Lemma 8.3, restriction of shift mass", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every label \\(q\\in A\\), \\( m_q(f) \\le 2^{2s} \\sum_{\\substack{\\widetilde q\\in\\widetilde A\\\\ \\rho(\\widetilde q)=q}} m_{\\widetilde q}(\\widetilde f). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\rho:\\widetilde A\\to A\\) be a surjective affine map with two-element fibers.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  H=\\operatorname{Aff}(A,\\mathbb F_2),\\qquad\n  \\widetilde H=\\operatorname{Aff}(\\widetilde A,\\mathbb F_2),\n  \\qquad\n  \\iota=\\rho^*:H\\hookrightarrow\\widetilde H.\n  \\]\n  Pullback is linear even for affine \\(\\rho\\), has image of codimension one, and satisfies \\(\\iota(1)=1\\). Its dual \\(\\iota^*:\\widetilde H^*\\to H^*\\) has two-element fibers and agrees with \\(\\rho\\) on evaluation functionals.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a row context and outside tables. Let \\(\\widetilde f(\\widetilde L,F_D)\\) be a function on \\(\\widetilde H^{2s}\\) and the slice tables.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose its shift Fourier support satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\widetilde Q_{g0}+\\widetilde Q_{g1}\n  =\n  \\widetilde Q_{h0}+\\widetilde Q_{h1}\n  \\qquad(g,h\\in[s]).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  f(L,F_D)=\\widetilde f(\\iota L,F_D),\n  \\]\n  with \\(\\iota\\) applied to each shift. Define masses by the common-pair-sum formula (7.1) in the respective row spaces.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every label \\(q\\in A\\),\n\\[\nm_q(f)\n\\le\n2^{2s}\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\widetilde q\\in\\widetilde A\\\\\n\\rho(\\widetilde q)=q}}\nm_{\\widetilde q}(\\widetilde f).\n\\tag{8.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed slice tables, Fourier inversion and orthogonality give\n\\[\n\\widehat f(Q)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\widetilde Q\\\\\n\\iota^*\\widetilde Q_{gb}=Q_{gb}\\ \\forall g,b}}\n\\widehat{\\widetilde f}(\\widetilde Q).\n\\]\nThe sum has \\(2^{2s}\\) indices, so its squared absolute value is at most \\(2^{2s}\\) times the sum of squared absolute values of the terms.</p><p>Sum over all \\(Q\\) with pair sums \\(q\\). Every nonzero coefficient on the right has a common pair sum \\(\\widetilde q\\) restricting to \\(q\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\widetilde q(1)\n=\n\\widetilde q(\\iota(1))\n=\nq(1)=1.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\widetilde q\\) is a label in \\(\\widetilde A\\), and \\(\\rho(\\widetilde q)=q\\). Every lifted index is counted only once. Average over slice tables to prove (8.8).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 8.4, decoding", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G) \\ge 2^{-2s}\\frac{p_*\\theta}{2}. \\) The decoding loss is independent of the alphabet dimension \\(n\\) and repetition length \\(T\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use an affine game \\(\\mathcal G\\) with label spaces \\(\\mathbb F_2^{n+1},\\mathbb F_2^n\\), the graph test of Proposition 4, and fixed arbitrary cut tables.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal B=(\\mathbb F_2^{n+1})^T,\\qquad\n  A=(\\mathbb F_2^n)^T,\n  \\]\n  and use \\(G_{\\mathbf e},\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}\\), free-shift coordinates, and masses as in Propositions 5, 10, and 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose the extraction conclusion (7.4) holds for \\(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}\\) with \\(p_*,\\theta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  T\\ge\\frac{2^{n(2k-2s)}}{(p_*\\theta)^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The coordinate change \\(R\\leftrightarrow(L,\\Gamma)\\) uses only additions of rows, depends only on the tree and selected node, and therefore commutes with affine pullback.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{Val}(\\mathcal G)\n\\ge\n2^{-2s}\\frac{p_*\\theta}{2}.\n\\tag{8.9}\n\\]\nThe decoding loss is independent of the alphabet dimension \\(n\\) and repetition length \\(T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 11–12 give the planted-law score bound (8.7). Construct randomized endpoint labelers using only their own endpoint and shared randomness. The entire game and cut tables are fixed background data; efficiency of these labelers is not needed to lower-bound game value.</p><p>Given a random input edge \\(e=(x,y)\\), choose a uniform public mark \\(a_*\\in[T]\\). At all other slots publicly sample independent edges, including endpoints and projections, and insert the input edge in the marked slot. Its projection is used only in the analysis, not revealed to either labeler. Conditional on any mark, the full tuple \\(\\mathbf e\\) has the product edge law, so the tuple is independent of the mark. Each labeler knows its own endpoint tuple and all unmarked edges.</p><p>Publicly sample depth, slice, node, outside tables, context, latent rows, and masks according to the planted experiment. This row sampling uses fixed alphabet dimensions, tree coordinates, and the mark, independently of the input edge. Together with \\(\\mathbf e\\), it has exactly the planted law of (8.7).</p><p>The \\(Y\\)-labeler forms \\(\\mathbf y\\), computes the fixed maximizing label \\(q^*\\) from (8.2), and outputs \\(q^*_{a_*}\\). All data needed are available: the conditional average defining \\(\\overline G_{\\mathbf y}\\) is determined by the game and cut tables. It does not use the actual marked edge or its projection.</p><p>For the \\(X\\)-labeler, replace the marked factor of \\(A\\) by \\(\\mathbb F_2^{n+1}\\):\n\\[\n\\widetilde A\n=\n\\prod_{a\\ne a_*}\\mathbb F_2^n\n\\times\\mathbb F_2^{n+1},\n\\]\nwith factors in original slot order. Factor the full projection as\n\\[\n\\mathcal B\\xrightarrow{\\kappa}\\widetilde A\n\\xrightarrow{\\rho}A.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\kappa\\) is the public edge projection in each unmarked slot and the identity in the marked slot; \\(\\rho\\) is identity in unmarked slots and the unknown \\(\\pi_e\\) in the marked slot. Thus \\(\\kappa\\) is known to the \\(X\\)-labeler.</p><p>Every row in \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(R^{\\mathrm{new}}\\) has zero linear coefficient block at the mark. The same holds for \\(E,E_\\Gamma\\), and\n\\[\n\\Gamma'=\\Gamma+E_\\Gamma,\n\\]\nsince masks and the coordinate change only multiply by scalars and add rows. Consequently\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\Gamma'=\\rho^*\\Gamma'\n\\]\nis known to the \\(X\\)-labeler: keep all unmarked and constant coefficients unchanged and set marked coefficients to zero. This does not require \\(\\pi_e\\), including its affine translation.</p><p>Put \\(\\widetilde H=\\operatorname{Aff}(\\widetilde A,\\mathbb F_2)\\). The \\(X\\)-labeler forms\n\\[\n\\widetilde G(\\widetilde R,F)\n=\n\\sigma_{\\mathbf x}^{\\mathrm o}\n\\bigl(W_{\\widetilde A}(\\widetilde R,F)\\circ\\kappa\\bigr),\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde R\\in\\widetilde H^k.\n\\]\nFix its context to \\(\\widetilde\\Gamma'\\), its outside tables to the public \\(F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\), and leave all \\(2s\\) shifts free. Let \\(\\widetilde m_{\\widetilde q}\\) be its masses. They are nonnegative and sum to at most one, by Parseval and \\(|\\widetilde G|\\le1\\).</p><p>Sample a label \\(\\widetilde q\\) with probability at least \\(\\widetilde m_{\\widetilde q}\\) for every label, assigning leftover probability to a fixed label. Use an additional independent public uniform random number, and output \\(\\widetilde q_{a_*}\\). This sampling does not use \\(q^*\\).</p><p>To check Proposition 13’s support hypothesis, take any \\(h\\in\\widetilde H\\). Shifting both child generators in one pair by \\(h\\) multiplies the output of the selected gate by \\((-1)^{h(\\widetilde l)}\\) at every label \\(\\widetilde l\\). Doing this in two distinct pairs leaves the gate output, and hence the word, unchanged. These operations translate only free shifts and preserve context. Character orthogonality gives a common pair sum for every shift index of \\(\\widetilde G\\), for every fixed set of tables.</p><p>Word generation commutes pointwise with pullback of rows. Since \\(\\pi_{\\mathbf e}=\\rho\\circ\\kappa\\), restricting all free shifts of \\(\\widetilde G\\) to \\(\\rho^*H\\) gives exactly \\(G_{\\mathbf e}\\), with context \\(\\Gamma'\\) and the same tables. The map \\(\\rho\\) has two-element fibers, so Proposition 13 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\rho(\\widetilde q)=q^*}\n\\widetilde m_{\\widetilde q}\n\\ge\n2^{-2s}\nm_{q^*}(G_{\\mathbf e};\\Gamma',F_{\\mathrm{out}}).\n\\]\nConditioned on planted data, the left side lower-bounds the probability that the sampled full label projects to \\(q^*\\). This implies satisfaction of the marked input edge. Averaging and using (8.7) gives success probability at least\n\\[\n2^{-2s}p_*\\theta/2.\n\\]</p><p>Finally fix all shared randomness, including the sampling uniform number. These same fixed data define the strategies at every endpoint and hence a deterministic labeling. The average value of these labelings has the displayed lower bound, so some fixed choice attains it.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma B.1, conversion to simple unweighted graphs", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One can construct, in deterministic polynomial time, a simple unweighted graph \\(G'\\) and an integer scale \\(Q\\) such that \\( \\left| \\frac{\\operatorname{MaxCut}(G')}{Q} \\operatorname{MaxCut}(G) \\right| \\le\\epsilon'. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be a graph on \\(N\\) vertices with nonnegative rational weights \\(w_{uv}\\) on unordered distinct vertex pairs, and\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{u&lt;v}w_{uv}\\le1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\epsilon'&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If the input has loops, delete them without renormalizing the other weights.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can construct, in deterministic polynomial time, a simple unweighted graph \\(G'\\) and an integer scale \\(Q\\) such that\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\frac{\\operatorname{MaxCut}(G')}{Q}\n-\n\\operatorname{MaxCut}(G)\n\\right|\n\\le\\epsilon'.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a prime\n\\[\np&gt;\\max\\{2,2N^2/\\epsilon'\\}.\n\\]\nA prime of polynomial size can be found deterministically by testing successive integers, with Bertrand’s postulate bounding the search.</p><p>Replace each vertex by a cluster indexed by \\(\\mathbb F_p^6\\). For \\(u&lt;v\\), let \\(I_{uv}\\subseteq\\mathbb F_p\\) be a fixed set of \\(\\lfloor pw_{uv}\\rfloor\\) residues, for example the first such residues in the usual ordering. Join \\(x\\) in cluster \\(u\\) to \\(y\\) in cluster \\(v\\) exactly when\n\\[\nx\\cdot y\\in I_{uv}.\n\\]\nThere are no within-cluster edges. This is a simple graph on \\(Np^6\\) vertices, constructible by enumerating pairs. Take \\(Q=p^{12}\\).</p><p>Fix \\(I\\subseteq\\mathbb F_p\\) and \\(S,T\\subseteq\\mathbb F_p^6\\). With \\(\\psi(a)=\\exp(2\\pi ia/p)\\), Fourier inversion gives\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_I(a)=\\sum_{h\\in\\mathbb F_p}\\beta_h\\psi(ha),\n\\qquad\n\\beta_h=\\frac1p\\sum_{a\\in I}\\psi(-ha),\n\\qquad |\\beta_h|\\le1.\n\\]\nFor \\(h\\ne0\\), the matrix \\(M_h(x,y)=\\psi(hx\\cdot y)\\) satisfies\n\\[\nM_hM_h^*=p^6I\n\\]\nby orthogonality, so its operator norm is \\(p^3\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|\n\\#\\{(x,y)\\in S\\times T:x\\cdot y\\in I\\}\n-\n\\frac{|I|}{p}|S||T|\n\\right|\n&amp;\\le\n\\sum_{h\\ne0}|\\beta_h|p^3\\sqrt{|S||T|}\\\\\n&amp;\\le p^{10}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor \\(I=I_{uv}\\), divide by \\(p^{12}\\) and use\n\\[\n\\left||I|/p-w_{uv}\\right|\\le1/p\n\\]\nto obtain\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\frac{e_{uv}(S,T)}{p^{12}}\n-\nw_{uv}\\frac{|S|}{p^6}\\frac{|T|}{p^6}\n\\right|\n\\le\\frac1p+\\frac1{p^2}.\n\\tag{B.1}\n\\]</p><p>Fix any cut of \\(G'\\), and let \\(a_u\\in[0,1]\\) be the positive-side fraction of cluster \\(u\\). Crossing edges between clusters \\(u,v\\) form two rectangles. Their scaled count differs from\n\\[\nw_{uv}\\bigl(a_u(1-a_v)+(1-a_u)a_v\\bigr)\n\\]\nby at most \\(2/p+2/p^2\\). Summing over at most \\(N(N-1)/2\\) pairs costs at most\n\\[\nN(N-1)(1/p+1/p^2).\n\\]\nThe sum of the displayed weighted expressions is the expected weight of a cut of \\(G\\) assigning vertex \\(u\\) a positive sign independently with probability \\(a_u\\). It is at most \\(\\operatorname{MaxCut}(G)\\). This gives the upper bound for every cut of \\(G'\\).</p><p>Conversely, assign every vertex in a cluster the sign of a maximum cut of \\(G\\). The same rectangle estimate gives the reverse inequality with the same error. The choice of \\(p\\) ensures\n\\[\nN(N-1)(1/p+1/p^2)&lt;\\epsilon'.\n\\]\nLoops have zero cut contribution and may be deleted initially.</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 17, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 10, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [4, 8, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 3, "cite"], [8, 3, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [10, 1, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 20, "cite"], [10, 23, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 1, "cite"], [11, 20, "cite"], [11, 23, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 18, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [14, 19, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"], [16, 1, "cite"], [16, 23, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 19, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 16, "part"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 23, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [23, 1, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"]]});
